\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Scale New Heights On US Debt Woes\b0\par\par\uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2011\par 07:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- U.S. dollar swoons against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains prompt Korea and \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a second straight day\par \par \uc2 -- Singapore dollar, Malaysian ringgit at long-term highs\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Martin Vaughan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  soared to new highs against the dollar Wednesday, as investors showed the greenback no mercy on increasing expectations the U.S. will lose its triple-A credit rating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell to Y77.78, its lowest since the post World War II-low of Y76.25 marked in March. Japan Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda reiterated that he will continue to closely monitor the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and South Korea for a second straight day \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from rising too quickly and damaging their export-dependent economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday's gains extended a months-long trend of investors piling into Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and out of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the dollar and euro, which are burdened by seemingly intractable fiscal problems.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Now more than ever, Asia is seen as a region where there are strong fundamentals," said Thomas Harr, head of Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Standard Chartered Bank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "These flows coming into Asia are safe-haven flows; they are increasing even though you could say that risk appetite is not very strong," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar hit an all-time low against the Singapore dollar, breaching the key psychological barrier of S$1.2000 to hit S$1.1992 before rebounding gently. The Malaysian ringgit was at its strongest in 14 years, as the greenback sank to MYR2.9350 from MYR2.9535 late Tuesday in Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar selling lifted the Indonesian rupiah to a seven-year high. A dealer said Bank Indonesia may be checking prices in the market but there was no evidence of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many market observers expect the U.S. to be hit by a ratings downgrade even if the White House and Congress agree on a deal to raise borrowing limits by Tuesday, a deal which for the moment remains elusive. Wednesday's dollar losses are in part front-running that expected downgrade, Harr said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The threat of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tempered investor enthusiasm in some markets. "With the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  present, punters are pushing the baht up with caution," said a Bangkok dealer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Aussie dollar, already riding high on the U.S. debt woes, hit a fresh 30-year high at $1.1062 after a stronger-than-expected consumer inflation reading. The Aussie was trading around $1.1055 at 0450 GMT, up from $1.0951 as Asian trading got underway.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro climbed to a three-week high at $1.4537 earlier in the day but eased back to $1.4520 at 0450 GMT. The common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was at Y113.01, unchanged from New York trade. The dollar was at Y77.82 from Y77.89.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                            Latest       Previous   %Chg    Daily    Daily   %Chg \par Dollar Rates                               Close            High      Low  12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Japan            77.82-84       77.86-91  -0.07    78.02    77.78  -4.06 \par EUR/USD Euro            1.4520-22      1.4509-12  +0.07   1.4536   1.4494  +8.44 \par GBP/USD U.K.            1.6429-34      1.6402-07  +0.17   1.6437   1.6406  +5.28 \par USD/CHF Switzerland     0.8019-22      0.8011-16  +0.08   0.8024   0.8008 -14.22 \par USD/CAD Canada          0.9429-34      0.9438-43  -0.09   0.9445   0.9430  -5.19 \par AUD/USD Australia       1.1052-56      1.0955-59  +0.88   1.1061   1.0944  +8.01 \par NZD/USD New Zealand     0.8747-52      0.8703-08  +0.51   0.8761   0.8700 +12.22 \par  \par Euro Rates \par  \par EUR/JPY Japan           113.01-04      112.98-03  -0.01   113.22   112.82  +4.04 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Martin Vaughan, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 6415 4033; martin.vaughan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Andrew Monahan in Tokyo and Phisanu Phromchanya in Bangkok contributed to this article [ 27-07-11 0558GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1034 1863 28038 4162   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110727e77r000ds\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
