\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Thai Finance Minister: Dollar To Keep Falling In Long-Term\b0\par\par\uc2 477 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2011\par 12:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By James Hookway, Leigh Murray and Almar Latour\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The U.S. dollar is expected to continue heading south over the long term as countries can't escape their economic fundamentals, Thai Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij said Friday.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. can only repay its massive debt by allowing depreciation of dollar, Korn told The Wall Street Journal and Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 "I see no way of repaying it other than through devaluation. Hopefully it will be gradual, but one can never predict that."\par \par \uc2 He said the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, both buying and selling the U.S. dollar, and it will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to limit any excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 However, he said there is no need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight the trend and that there is no evidence of any "hot money" behind the baht's recent strength.\par \par \uc2 "So far they (the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 ) have been able to manage excess volatility," Korn said.\par \par \uc2 He said Thai exporters have been extremely resilient to the baht's strength, though they need any appreciation to be gradual.\par \par \uc2 "Exporters detest volatility the most," Korn said.\par \par \uc2 The minister called on Thai exporters to diversify their trade settlement \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , into yuan, yen or other units, to match their trading counterparties in order to reduce dependency on the U.S. dollar, which is on the descent.\par \par \uc2 He noted that the adoption of the Chinese yuan in international trade and investment has been happening faster than expected.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Korn said the strong baht will likely attract more investments for value-added projects than those seeking the cheapest production base and low labor costs.\par \par \uc2 Potential further increases in global oil prices are considered the biggest threat to Thailand's economy, as they will fuel inflationary pressures, he said.\par \par \uc2 Higher oil and commodity prices are key factors pushing up production costs and inflation. However, the global food crisis could provide an obvious opportunity for Thailand as a food producing country.\par \par \uc2 Korn said the government's proposed minimum wage hike won't put upward pressure on inflation, which is driven more by external factors.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat Party-led government has been floating the idea of raising minimum wage by another 25% in two years in a bid to increase the purchasing power of low income earners. The proposal comes ahead of general elections in the country, which could take place by late June or early July.\par \par \uc2 Korn said the poll result will likely to be a close race between the ruling Democrat Party and the Puea Thai Party, backed by fugitive ex-Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra.\par \par \uc2 -By James Hookway, Leigh Murray and Almar Latour, +66 2690 4200; leigh.murray@dowjones.com [ 29-04-11 1037GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110429e74t000sl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
