\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Shares Mostly Higher; Fed Stance Buoys Risk Appetite\b0\par\par\uc2 1,054 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2011\par 07:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 By Shri Navaratnam and Puja Rajeev\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets were mostly higher Thursday, while the U.S. dollar fell across the board, as the U.S. Federal Reserve's commitment to retain its ultra-easy monetary policy buoyed sentiment and drove riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  up.\par \par \uc2 The ongoing earnings season also played a big hand in Tokyo, Seoul and Sydney markets.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was up 1.3%, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was 0.4% higher, South Korea's Kospi Composite was off 0.1%, Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index tacked on 0.5% and China's Shanghai Composite was off 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were up 34 points in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street's surge Wednesday bolstered regional sentiment, after the Federal Open Market Committee signaled the end of its controversial $600 billion bond buying program as planned, and Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep interest rates accommodative. He said there was no specific time frame to hike interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "The comments from Bernanke are very bullish for equity markets, via a weak U.S. dollar, U.S. earnings and commodity prices," said RBS Morgans investment adviser Chris MacDonald in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 The news spurred risk appetite, pushing stocks, gold and oil prices higher. Risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged, with the euro and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tapping multi-year highs against the U.S. dollar. Central banks in Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to protect their export-led economies.\par \par \uc2 Gold bulls took a breather after earlier driving spot gold to a record high $1,530.30 a troy ounce; the yellow metal was recently fetching $1,526.90, down 40 cents from its New York settlement Wednesday. Oil prices remained bid, with June Nymex crude oil futures up 48 cents at $113.24 per barrel on Globex, off its 31-month high of $113.70 tapped in early trade.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, strong earnings offset concerns over grim March industrial production data.\par \par \uc2 Industrial production dropped a record 15.3% in March, but Japanese manufacturers expect their output to rise 3.9% on-month in April and increase 2.7% in May.\par \par \uc2 "The market seems to be reacting more favorably to earnings than we had anticipated (and) expectations are growing that Japan will display its underlying strength through speedy recovery efforts," said Cosmo Securities equity strategist Toshikazu Horiuchi.\par \par \uc2 Kyocera rose 2.6% after reporting strong fiscal fourth-quarter earnings and Advantest jumped 5.2% after posting a sharply narrower loss in its fiscal fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 Sony\uc2  extended its losses on news Wednesday that its online video game network had been hacked; the stock fell 3.1%.\par \par \uc2 A handful of profit warnings and a strong Australian dollar tempered gains in Sydney. The Australian dollar hit a new 29-year high of US$1.0903 in early trade, pressuring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sensitive stocks such as \uc2 Westfield Group\uc2  and \uc2 Bluescope Steel\uc2 , which fell 0.7%, and 2.6%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Goodman Fielder\uc2  and Whitehaven were hit by profit warnings, falling 8.6% and 3.1% respectively.\par \par \uc2 In China, the benchmark Shanghai Composite Index gave up morning gains. The reversal was triggered by sharp losses for the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated B shares, which were hurt by a weaker U.S. dollar and lingering concerns over China's launch of an international board.\par \par \uc2 "The B share market was designed to attract foreign investors and the international board will definitely attract many of the B share's target investors," said Duan Junfang at Dongfang Securities.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai B Share Subindex was down 4.1%. \uc2 Wuhan Iron & Steel\uc2  was down 0.6% and \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  fell 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 The Seoul market turned lower as investors booked profits in heavyweight technology and construction stocks. Still, a few other stocks rose on earnings-linked news.\par \par \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  tacked on 3.8%, buoyed by expectations of a firm turnaround in second-quarter earnings after the tech giant Wednesday reported an unexpected first-quarter loss. \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  rose 2.5% after the world's second-largest producer of computer memory chips gave an upbeat outlook, and topped market expectations for its first quarter net profit, which fell 66% from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Among other markets, New Zealand's NZX-50 was 0.4% higher, Singapore's Straits Times Index gained 0.5%, Malaysia's KLCI rose 0.3%, Taiwan's Taiex fell 0.2%, Indonesian shares edged up 0.3% and Philippine shares slipped 0.5%. India's Sensex fell 0.1% and Thailand's SET was flat.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar continued to suffer against its major rivals after slipping sharply on Wednesday. Emmanuel Ng, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2 , said: "Dollar weakness is looking pervasive, while risk appetite remains strong."\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at $1.4862 against the dollar, its highest level since December 2009, from $1.4788 late in New York on Wednesday. It was at Y121.59 against the yen, from Y121.44. The dollar was buying Y81.81, compared with Y82.17.\par \par \uc2 Taking advantage of the greenback's woes, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared. In addition to the Australian dollar's fresh 29-year highs, the Singapore dollar hit a record S$1.2276 high against the US dollar and the Korean won rocketed to a fresh 32-month high at KRW1,071.90.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bond futures were up 0.05 at 139.99 points, after the Bank of Japan left its unsecured overnight call loan rate target unchanged in a range of 0.0%-0.1%, as expected, and finalized a lending facility to help sustain money flow following the March 11 earthquake.\par \par \uc2 -Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4142; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 28-04-11 0508GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110428e74s000er\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
