\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2011\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA, TAIWAN BANKING REGULATORS TO MEET TWICE A YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The banking regulators of China and Taiwan said Monday their chiefs have agreed to meet twice a year to discuss the regulation of Taiwan banks in China and vice versa, while lower-level exchanges between the two will be conducted as often as needed. (published 0226 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: S KOREA MAY TIGHTEN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RULES FURTHER THAN EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government said Tuesday it may tighten rules on banks' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions further than expected, in a strengthened effort to shore up the financial system and help shield the economy from speculative \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flows. (published 0243 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANK OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 THAILAND\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : HAVE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENED\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET TO CURB BAHT'S RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to curb the baht's rise, but the move has been to only try and limit short-term volatility, Gov. Prasarn Trairatvorakul said Tuesday. (published 0234 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: ASIA OUTPUT GROWTH MAY BE CUT BY UP TO 1.5 PERCENTAGE POINTS IN 2011 - ADB \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a report, published Tuesday, the bank urged governments not to aggravate conditions by imposing export bans on food products, and stressed the need to reduce speculative activities in food markets, and to avoid policies that restrict the transfer of food from surplus to deficit regions. (published 0257 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA ISSUES PROPOSED INCOME TAX LAW REVISIONS FOR PUBLIC FEEDBACK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's legislature has issued the full text of its proposed individual income tax law revisions for public feedback until May 31, the National People's Congress said Monday. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER REITERATES TAX OVERHAUL PLAN TO BE READY BY JUNE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister said Tuesday the government still aims to draft a long-term plan by the end of June to overhaul the nation's strained tax and social security systems, even as officials appear preoccupied with dealing with the aftermath of a major earthquake. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA BANKS MAY TAKE ON KRW400BLN IN REAL ESTATE LOANS - FSS OFFICIAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's 18 domestic commercial banks are looking at taking on a combined KRW400 billion in real-estate project financing loans held by the troubled savings bank sector, a senior official at the local financial watchdog said Tuesday. (published 0251 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANK OF THAILAND\f28 \uc2 \f2 : CAPITAL MOVEMENT LIKELY TO CONTINUE IN 2H \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  Gov. Prasarn Trairatvorakul said Tuesday that capital movements are likely to continue in the second half of this year. (published 0253 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: SPEAKER BOEHNER: LOWERING SUBSIDIES TO OIL FIRMS SHOULD BE 'LOOKED AT' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal government subsidies paid to big oil companies should be "looked at" as lawmakers strive to tackle federal budget deficits, House Speaker John Boehner said Monday. (published 0110 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: MIE HOLDINGS PLANS DEBUT GLOBAL DOLLAR BOND AFTER ROADSHOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MIE Holdings Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Tuesday it plans to sell U.S. dollar-denominated bonds to finance acquisitions, repay debt as well as for working capital and general corporate purposes. (published 0128 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK OF SHANGHAI PLANS TO SELL UP TO CNY5 BLN BONDS IN NEAR TERM -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Shanghai\f28 \uc2 \f2  Co. plans to sell up to CNY5 billion worth of subordinated bonds on the interbank market in the near term to supplement its capital, a person familiar with the situation said Tuesday. (published 0134 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par PREVIEW: NEW ZEALAND \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LIKELY TO HOLD CASH RATE AT 2.50% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of New Zealand is expected to hold the Official Cash Rate at 2.50% Thursday, after cutting the rate last month to counter the effects of a 6.3-magnitude earthquake that devastated New Zealand's second-largest city of Christchurch. (published 0239 GMT) [ 26-04-11 0300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110426e74q0005b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
