\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Thailand Former PM Thaksin: Will Cut Corporate Tax If Allies Win Election\b0\par\par\uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2011\par 18:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -Thailand former PM Thaksin says will cut corporate tax if allies win election\par \par \uc2 -Thailand's Thaksin says Thailand should adopt low-tax regime\par \par \uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Thaksin suggests less \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow baht rise\par \par \uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Thaksin: Stimulate local economy to generate more imports and curb baht\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By James Hookway \par    Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's ousted former leader Thaksin Shinawatra on Thursday said he will play a key role in setting economic policy if the country's main opposition party wins fresh elections planned for later this year, and that one of his first moves would be to cut corporate tax rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an interview with The Wall Street Journal, the telecommunications mogul-turned-politician said Thailand's 30% corporate tax rate was uncompetitive and would be lowered if the For Thais Party backed by Thaksin and his supporters win the vote, which could be held as soon as the end of June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We want to reduce companies' tax costs because this will enable them to pay higher salaries," Thaksin said. "And when employees get paid more they consume more, and when they consume more, we'll collect more Value Added Tax."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's corporate tax rates are comparatively high. Elsewhere in Southeast Asia, Malaysia's corporate tax rate is 25% and Singapore's is capped at 17%. The current Thai government has also said it might consider lowering corporate tax rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Definitely, when For Thais becomes the government, Thailand will become a low-tax regime because I see many countries do that quite effectively," Thaksin said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thaksin led Thailand from 2001 until 2006, when he was overthrown in a military coup. He now lives overseas to evade imprisonment on a 2008 corruption conviction which he says was politically motivated. A pioneer of populist, pro-business politics in Thailand, he is the only Thai leader to have been re-elected but remains a divisive figure in the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a potentially important economic adviser, Thaksin also said he would encourage \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to refrain from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively to slow the rise of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's baht \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the dollar by buying U.S. dollars and selling Thai baht. He said the country already had "excessive" reserves of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which could be better used elsewhere.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, Thaksin said he will focus on policies to strengthen the domestic economy and generate more imports to help offset the country's large trade surplus. "When imports and exports are quite close, then you'll see the baht devalue," Thaksin said. "We have to let our baht reflect our real economy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's foreign reserves stood at $181.5 billion as of March 25.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Thai baht was trading at 30.07 baht to the U.S. dollar in late Asian trading Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By James Hookway, The Wall Street Journal [ 04-07-11 1231ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110407e747000fv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
