\par\par \b \uc2 DJ INTERVIEW: Japan Disaster Won't Hurt Thai Economy - BOT Governor\b0\par\par\uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 10:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts lead, adds context in the 4th-5th paragraphs, Parsarn comments on inflation, policy rates in the 9th-17th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 --Japan disaster won't have significant impact on Thai economy.\par \par \uc2 --Japan may further diversify production bases, could benefit \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 --Baht level remains appropriate, less need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 --Plans to further relax regulations to facilitate capital outflows. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Leigh Murray and Phisanu Phromchanya \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai economy won't suffer any significant impact from Japan's earthquake and tsunami and could benefit long-term as Japanese companies might further diversify their manufacturing bases overseas, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  Gov. Prasarn Trairatvorakul said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the short-term, Thai exporters may see increased demand as the quake-stricken country would need to import more food and goods as it recovers, Prasarn told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. However, he added that more exports aren't expected to substantially boost Thailand's overall shipments, just over 10% of which go to Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prasarn said the bank is still assessing the impact on foreign direct investment from Japan, but is maintaining its 2011 economic growth forecast at 3.0% to 5.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan is the largest investor in Thailand in terms of FDI, and Southeast Asia's second-largest economy has become a regional manufacturing hub for many Japanese companies, including auto giants such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (7203.TO), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mazda Motor Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (7261.TO), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Motors Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (7211.TO), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (7267.TO), and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (7201.TO).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese investors last year applied for tax privileges for 363 projects with a total investment value of THB104.42 billion ($3.43 billion), more than any other country, according to the Board of Investment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the downside, Thailand's tourism industry may suffer a decline in Japanese visitors in the near-term, said Prasarn. Japan was the third-largest source of tourists for Thailand last year, according to Kasikorn Research Center.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prasarn said the Japan disaster will likely have a limited impact on Asia's growth as Japan's economy isn't a significant contributor to regional growth, though it will likely bolster commodity prices in the short-term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said a spike in remittances of Japanese yen in the wake of the disaster may push the yen's value higher in the short-run, but it's expected to depreciate in the long run as economic fundamentals weaken.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation remains a major threat to Thailand's economy, even though it is still at a "manageable level," Prasarn said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's consumer price index rose 2.87% in February from a year earlier. Core CPI, which excludes volatile energy and food prices, rose 1.45% from a year earlier, still within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 0.5% to 3.0% target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prasarn expects price pressures to accelerate when government measures to aid the cost of living expire later this year, and said there's a fair chance core inflation will temporarily breach the 3.0% level in the fourth quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said the Monetary Policy Committee will continue its rate normalization efforts to tackle inflation and rising inflation expectations, noting that gradual rate increase is preferred over an aggressive front-load tightening of 50 basis points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Judging from the two previous meetings, I think the majority of members wouldn't support the jump, it's not necessary," Prasarn said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The MPC--which has raised its benchmark rate 125 basis points since last July, including a quarter percentage point increase last week--will consider the impact from the Japan disaster at its April 20 meeting, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is aiming to raise interest rates until the output gap--the difference between Thailand's actual output and full potential output--is closed, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We want to achieve a rate that should not exert any bias on either growth or inflation," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although a lengthy period of negative real interest rates could adversely affect the economy and distort market mechanisms, it's not a key determining factor for raising rates, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prasarn said Thailand's trade account is expected to swing to a surplus in February, with January's $588 million deficit likely a one-off, caused by hefty gold imports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A trade surplus of around $4 billion to $5 billion is expected this year, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Prasarn said the Thai baht is currently at an appropriate level and moving in line with fundamentals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market "once in awhile", there is less need this year as the baht appears to be moving in a two-way direction, said Prasarn. The dollar fell by 10.6% against the baht last year, despite hefty \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , due mainly to massive capital inflows into Asia's emerging markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  is planning to further relax regulations to facilitate capital outflows to better balance the movement of capital flows, he said, without elaborating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank stands ready to tighten restrictions on capital inflows, although the move isn't currently needed, said Prasarn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Leigh Murray and Phisanu Phromchanya, Dow Jones Newswires; 66 2690 4200; leigh.murray@dowjones.com, phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 15, 2011 05:00 ET (09:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110315e73f001w8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
