\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Disaster Won't Hurt Thai Economy - BOT Governor\b0\par\par\uc2 607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds Prasarn comments on impact on Asian growth, Thai tourism, trade account, baht level and regulations on capital flows from the 6th paragraph)\par \par \uc2 --Japan disaster won't have significant impact on Thai economy.\par \par \uc2 --Japan may further diversify production bases, could benefit \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 --Baht level remains appropriate, less need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 --Plans to further relax regulations to facilitate capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Leigh Murray and Phisanu Phromchanya \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects no significant impact on the country's economy from Japan's earthquake and tsunami, and is maintaining its economic growth forecast for this year at 3.0% to 5.0%, Gov. Prasarn Trairatvorakul said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the bank is still assessing the impact on foreign direct investment from Japan, Thailand's largest investor in terms of FDI, Prasarn told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He expects Thai exports to Japan to increase in the short term as the quake-stricken country would need to import more food and goods, but that isn't expected to substantially boost Thailand's overall shipments, just over 10% of which go to Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the disaster may encourage Japanese firms to further diversify their production bases, which Thailand will stand to benefit from, said Prasarn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The spike in remittances of Japanese yen in the wake of the disaster may push the value of the Japanese unit higher in the short-run, but the yen is expected to depreciate in the long run as economic fundamentals weaken, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The disaster in Japan will likely have limited impact on Asia's growth prospects as Japan's economy isn't a significant contributor to regional growth in the first place, though it will likely bolster commodity prices in the short-term, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's tourism industry will likely to be dampened by a decline in Japanese travelers in the near-term, said Prasarn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan was the third largest source of tourists for Thailand last year, after Malaysian and Chinese visitors, according to Kasikorn Research Center.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's trade account is expected to swing back to a surplus in February, Prasarn said, adding that January's $588 million deficit is likely a one-off, caused by hefty gold imports during the month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand is expected to record a trade surplus of around $4 billion to $5 billion this year, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Prasarn said the Thai baht is currently at an appropriate level and is moving in line with fundamentals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hasn't detected any unusual capital flows so far this year, though it has stepped into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market "once in a while" to curb excessive volatility and ensure that the local unit isn't out of line with its regional peers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sees a lesser need for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year as the baht appears to be moving in a two-way direction due to various local and external factors, said Prasarn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell by 10.6% against the baht last year, despite hefty \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , due mainly to massive capital inflows into emerging markets in Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  is planning to further relax regulations to facilitate capital outflows to better balance the movement of capital flows, he said, without elaborating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank is standing ready to impose tighter restrictions on capital inflows in case it's needed though it the move is "not called for" currently, said Prasarn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Leigh Murray and Phisanu Phromchanya, Dow Jones Newswires; 66 2690 4200; leigh.murray@dowjones.com [ 15-03-11 0705GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110315e73f000h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
