\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Peso, baht down after Japan earthquake\b0\par\par\uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Peso down 0.4 pct after big quake in Japan\par \par \uc2  * USD/KRW 1-mth NDF up to 1,130\par \par \uc2  * Quake accelerates risk reduction \par \par \uc2 (Updates with text, prices)\par \par \uc2  By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 11 (Reuters) - The Philippine peso and the Thai baht fell against the dollar on Friday, extending losses after a powerful earthquake hit northeast Japan, triggering a 10-metre tsunami prompted investors to cut positions in the riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Earlier China's stronger-than-expected output data alleviated investors' concerns over the health of the world's No. 2 economy after it reported weak February figures. \par \par \uc2  But news of the massive quake hit the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "That is another reason to encourage a risk-off environment," said Pin Ru Tan, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rates strategist at the \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2  The yen extended losses against the dollar and the euro while Nikkei futures in Singapore tumbled more than 3 percent.\par \par \uc2  Before the earthquake, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut some losses as China data reassured investors that Asia would continue to grow faster than developed markets.\par \par \uc2  "The quake will prevent investors from buying Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more although Asia looks best in the longer term," said Jeong My-young, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist of Samsung Futures in Seoul.\par \par \uc2  PESO\par \par \uc2  The peso weakened past 43.60 per dollar where the Philippine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been spotted selling dollars to check the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's falls.\par \par \uc2  Investors rushed to cover dollar-short positions after the quake.\par \par \uc2  A Manila-based dealer said he has not seen dollar-sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet.\par \par \uc2  WON\par \par \uc2  The one-month dollar/won non-deliverable forward (NDF) rose to as high as 1,130 after the earthquake.\par \par \uc2  Earlier, the spot won eased, but found strong support from exporters' demand for settlements and as dealers and corporates were wary of officials stepping into the market to support the won around 1,130 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Inflation is the biggest issue now and (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities) will probably sell dollars even when it gets close to 1,130," said a senior foreign bank dealer in Seoul.\par \par \uc2  Dealers say South Korean authorities entered the market in February to keep a lid on the won, which has been bouncing around in a 1,104-1,134 range so far this year.\par \par \uc2  BAHT\par \par \uc2  The baht reversed gains on a bout of risk aversion and very aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought at least $500 million between 30.25-30.24 in the previous session.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR                                  \par \par \uc2  Change on the day at 0700 GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        83.25          82.92       -0.40\par  Sing dlr        1.2735         1.2727       -0.06\par  Taiwan dlr      29.580         29.5