\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Central Banks  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  As  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Rise; Yuan At Record High\b0\par\par\uc2 By Arran Scott, Aries Poon and Ditas Lopez  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2010\par 12:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Central banks in South Korea, Malaysia and \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Thursday as Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged against the dollar on optimism about the region's economic outlook, underscored by data showing the Chinese industrial sector expanded strongly in December.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan, meanwhile, unveiled measures to buttress its banking system against rapid movements in foreign capital, the latest Asian country to introduce stricter regulations to control the risks posed by so-called "hot money" flows.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were exagerated by thin trading conditions with many investors away for year-end holidays. But traders said an upward trend for most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears set to continue, with China's decision to guide the yuan to a modern record high against the dollar cementing the bullish sentiment.\par \par \uc2 "People are making a bet that growth in emerging markets will still be on an uptrend and that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue with their appreciation," said Lum Choong Kuan, head of fixed income research at CIMB Group in Kuala Lumpur. "With the debt crisis in Europe and with the U.S. still showing protracted slow growth, investors will have no other place to put their money but here."\par \par \uc2 Capital has been flooding into Asia this year, helping to finance investments in one of the world's fastest-growing regions. But the influx of foreign money has raised concerns in Asian capitals about the perils of fast-moving capital flows, given the damage the region's economies suffered during the Asian financial crisis of the late 1990s, when a previous boom ended suddenly and foreigners rushed for the exits.\par \par \uc2 Fresh data Thursday from China, the powerhouse of the Asian economy, showed manufacturing growth eased slightly in December but was still at relatively high levels. The HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, fell to 54.4 in December from 55.3 in November; any reading above 50 indicates expansion.\par \par \uc2 Hongbin Qu, \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's chief economist for China, said that despite the moderation in the PMI inflation remains Beijing's top policy concern, rather than economic growth.\par \par \uc2 He expects China to take further tightening measures to curb inflation--which hit 5.1% in November--including additional modest interest rate hikes. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates Dec. 25, its second hike in less than three months.\par \par \uc2 CIMB's Lum said central banks in emerging markets are looking at tightening monetary policy next year, which means interest-rate differentials between emerging markets and the developed economies of the U.S., Europe and Japan should grow wider. That leaves Asian central banks looking for ways to control inflows of capital chasing higher returns.\par \par \uc2 In Kuala Lumpur, traders said Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying dollars to curb a rise in the ringgit, which hit a three-month high Thursday. \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  may have bought dollars around MYR3.0810-MYR3.0820, dealers said. The dollar was last at MYR3.0846.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Seoul said they suspected the Bank of Korea entered the market, buying more than $500 million at intervals around KRW1,135, KRW1,136, and KRW1,140. The dollar closed at KRW1,134.80, bringing the won's gains to 2.6% for the year.\par \par \uc2 In Bangkok, the dollar was last at THB30.15--down from THB30.16 late Wednesday--with suspected buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  around that level to limit the downside, two dealers said.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the dollar/baht will move much on the final trading day of this year, especially with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  supporting the pair," one Bangkok-based dealer said.\par \par \uc2 In Taipei, which has been trying to temper gains in the New Taiwan dollar--a favorite among investors seeking exposure to China, given Taiwan's increasingly close economic ties to the mainland--the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of China (Taiwan) announced new measures to control capital flows.\par \par \uc2 Starting Saturday, local banks will have to set aside 90.00% of foreign investors' new deposits as reserves, a quantum leap from the current level of 9.775%.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also raised its benchmark interest rate by 12.5 basis points, the third hike this year as it seeks to normalize lending costs and cool a sizzling property market. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Perng Fai-nan said inflation will likely accelerate next year.\par \par \uc2 Also Thursday, Taiwan's financial regulator also said it will inspect banks' use of foreign funds to determine whether there has been speculative trading in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Earlier Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of China (Taiwan) said it had found "unusual" trading strategies at a small number of banks.\par \par \uc2 "Over the past few days, a small number of authorized \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks have targeted specific times of the day to sell off large quantities of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "Our inquiry has revealed that the funding for these transactions originates exclusively from the inward remittance made by certain foreign investors," the bank said, adding that it "asked the authorities concerned to probe into the final beneficiaries behind these foreign investors."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't name the banks in question or give details on the timing of the trades.\par \par \uc2 It's not unusual for Asian central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when volatility in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises. Many have taken regulatory steps in recent months to try to slow capital inflows and make their banking systems more resilient to movements in foreign funds.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would increase the amount of reserves commercial banks must hold at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  against foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits, among others steps. Also Wednesday, South Korea said it would announce lower ceilings on banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward positions in January.\par \par \uc2 For many countries, the main consideration has been to make sure their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't rise so much that they hurt export industries. Still, stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reduce the cost of imports, tempering inflationary pressures that are on the rise regionally on surging commodities prices. For that reason, many economists expect authorities to permit their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise further.\par \par \uc2 An official at the People's Bank of China Thursday echoed that sentiment, saying that a small, gradual appreciation in the yuan presents more benefits to the Chinese economy than costs.\par \par \uc2 In an article in the Financial News, Sheng Songcheng, director of the PBOC's Statistics and Analysis Department, said the yuan's rise against the dollar since the exchange rate was made more flexible has had only a small impact on employment and growth. At the same time, Sheng wrote, the yuan's gradual rise has cut import costs, reduced inflationary pressures and prompted Chinese companies to become more competitive.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has appreciated 3.4% since June 19, when China pledged to increase the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading flexibility, effectively ending a two-year-long peg to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The State Council, or China's Cabinet, has the final say on major policies such as yuan reform. But Sheng's article, which featured prominently in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -run newspaper, may reflect growing confidence that Beijing can allow the yuan to rise further without severe consequences.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a record low against the yuan Thursday after the PBOC, which tightly controls the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , set the dollar/yuan parity rate at an all-time low of 6.6229, down from Wednesday's rate of 6.6247. It marked the eighth straight session that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set the parity rate lower.\par \par \uc2 In the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.6008 around 0830 GMT, down from Wednesday's close of CNY6.6212. The dollar traded Thursday between CNY6.6000--the lowest intraday level since the yuan began regularly trading in 1994--and CNY6.6232.\par \par \uc2 The impact of yuan demand from bank customers was exaggerated by the thin year-end trading, one Shanghai-based trader at a local bank said.\par \par \uc2 "The market is rather one-sided right now," he said. "The yuan's gain today is definitely bigger than what people had anticipated."\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell against its major rivals, as U.S. Treasury bond yields fell overnight following strong demand at a seven-year auction, again exaggerated by a thin market. The dollar slipped to a seven-week low of Y81.28 and a fresh all-time low of CHF0.9418.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also was weaker against its Australian and New Zealand counterparts: The Australian dollar surged to a fresh post-float high of $1.0197, and the Kiwi dollar rose to a high of $0.7691.\par \par \uc2 Many participants "dumped quite a few (U.S.) dollars," said Satoshi Tate, senior vice president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  division at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4042; arran.scott@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Aaron Back in Beijing, Joy Shaw in Shanghai, Min-Jeong Lee in Seoul, Ankur Relia in Kuala Lumpur and Oranan Paweewun in Bangkok contributed to this article. [ 30-12-10 1121GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1184 4253 4258 5095  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101230e6cu000ib\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
