\par\par \b \uc2 Symphony Int Hdgs Portfolio Update\b0\par\par\uc2 2,642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2010\par 12:39\par \uc2 Regulatory News Service\par \uc2 RNS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par \par TIDMSIHL \par  \par RNS Number : 0140V \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Symphony International Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par 26 October 2010 \par  \par ? \par Not for Distribution, directly or indirectly, in or into the United States or \par any jurisdiction in which such distribution would be unlawful. \par  \par                       \f28 \uc2 \f2 SYMPHONY INTERNATIONAL HOLDINGS LTD\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par                                SHAREHOLDER UPDATE \par                             RELEASED 26 October 2010 \par  \par The financial markets in Asia continue to improve. However there remain a number \par of risks that could increase volatility in the medium term and impede growth. \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Symphony International Holdings Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 's ("SIHL" or the "Company") Net Asset \par Value ("NAV") increased from US$347,677,450 to US$410,190,151 between 30 June \par 2010 and 30 September 2010, or from US$1.0278 to US$1.1981 per share. This \par increase is largely attributable to improving market valuations of our listed \par investments and appreciation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Singapore and Thailand, where \par SIHL's portfolio companies predominantly operate. SIHL's NAV per share increase \par of 16.6% in 3Q10 compared favorably with select indices such as MSCI AC World, \par MSCI AC Asia and MSCI Singapore, which increased by 13.8%, 10.3% and 8.1%, \par respectively. MSCI Thailand performed better in 3Q10 with an increase of 22.7%. \par  \par PORTFOLIO SUMMARY \par  \par SIHL's NAV was US$410.19 million at 30 September 2010 and consisted of \par investments in the following segments: \par  \par Healthcare: US$43.41 million (10.6% of NAV) \par Hospitality: US$116.48 million (28.4% of NAV) \par Lifestyle: US$11.18 million (2.7% of NAV) \par Lifestyle / Real estate: US$123.88 million (30.2% of NAV) \par Temporary investments: US$115.25 million (28.1% of NAV). Temporary investments \par include cash and equivalents and are net of accounts receivable and payable \par  \par SIHL's NAV performed better than selected indices since SIHL's initial public \par offering in August 2007 through 30 September 2010. SIHL NAV has outperformed the \par MSCI AC World, MSCI AC Asia, MSCI Thailand and MSCI Singapore indices by 51.9%, \par 46.8%, 4.5% and 38.2%, respectively (Source: MSCI Inc., Company analysis). \par  \par SIHL's share price at 30 September 2010 was US$0.68 representing a 43.2% \par discount to NAV. \par  \par SIHL's NAV at 30 September 2010 consisted of listed investments (38.9% of NAV), \par unlisted investments (33.0% of NAV) and temporary investments (28.1% of NAV). \par Temporary investments include cash and equivalents and are net of accounts \par receivable and payable \par  \par  \par OVERVIEW \par  \par SIHL's listed investments accounted for 38.9% of NAV at 30 September 2010, down \par from 53.7% at 30 June 2010 primarily due to the sale of shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Parkway \par Holdings Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("Parkway"). On a per share basis, the value of SIHL's listed \par investments stood at US$0.466. Unlisted investments (including property) \par comprised a further 33.0% of SIHL's NAV (or US$0.395 per share), with the \par remaining 28.1% of NAV (or US$0.337 per share) being temporary investments. \par  \par The Parkway exit during 3Q10 was SIHL's first realization and was made in \par response to a General Offer from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Khazanah Nasional Berhad\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the Malaysian \par government's investment vehicle) to privatize the company. This followed a \par bidding war for the company, which resulted in an exit price representing a 29% \par premium to the average price over a 6-month period prior to the initial offer. \par SIHL's investment in Parkway generated a 21.6% annualised return and a gain of \par US$26.6 million. \par  \par Following the Parkway exit SIHL now has more than US$115 million temporary \par investments. Management continues to evaluate opportunities to deploy these \par funds in its areas of deal focus. \par  \par SIHL's share price continued to trade at a discount to NAV during 3Q10. At 30 \par September 2010, SIHL's share price was US$0.68, representing a discount to NAV \par of 43.2%. \par  \par There has been an improvement in financial markets on account of a more \par favourable outlook for the global economy as fears over a 'double-dip' \par recession, credit worthiness of sovereign debt and fiscal sustainability abate. \par The International Monetary Fund ("IMF") announced the world economy expanded at \par an annual rate of about 5.25% during the first half of 2010 in its October 2010 \par World Economic Outlook update, compared with an estimate of 5.00% in July. This \par growth was driven by a strong rebound in industrial production and trade on \par account of a surge in inventory and fixed investments. Asian economies in \par particular have been beneficiaries of the improved economic environment with \par output expanding above pre-crisis levels. The IMF estimates Emerging Asia \par (excludes developed Asian countries that include Japan, Australia and New \par Zealand) will grow at 9.2% in 2010 compared with 2.7% for developed economies. \par  \par Despite a general consensus that the recovery is well on track, underlying risks \par remain elevated, particularly in developed economies. The US and Europe are \par expected to experience relatively sluggish growth compared with emerging and \par developing economies due to high unemployment, continued balance sheet \par reparation and large budget deficits. Under a renewed stress scenario, \par refinancing of sovereign and bank debt could be difficult given the diminished \par capability of many developed economies to use liquidity buffers to restore \par confidence. The stronger investor sentiment towards faster growing emerging and \par developing economies has led to strong capital inflows, particularly in Asia. \par  \par There has been a strong appreciation in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and asset prices in most \par Asian countries. The relatively stronger economic position of Asian economies \par and expected long term interest rate differential compared with developed \par economies has attracted substantial capital. Thailand and Singapore, where \par SIHL's portfolio companies predominantly operate, have seen their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par strengthen by 10.11% and 4.75% against the US dollar in the first three quarters \par of 2010. With strong capital inflows and robust domestic demand, there is \par concern that asset price bubbles are emerging. \par  \par Export dependent Asian economies are looking to balance the negative impact of \par rising \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  while on the other hand managing inflationary pressures. \par Countries including \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , South Korea, China and the Philippines have \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciating, either through capital controls or \par increasing foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. Where there has been previously clear \par policies of removing accommodative monetary policy to reduce inflationary \par pressures, it appears that central banks of Asian economies have paused in \par raising interest rates to hinder \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from strengthening further. \par  \par We remain optimistic on the outlook for Asian economies, such as Singapore and \par Thailand, but there remain potential risks that could increase volatility in \par financial markets and hinder growth. The balancing of capital inflows, \par appreciating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and inflationary pressures in Asia will be a key concern \par in 2011. \par  \par  \par PORTFOLIO DEVELOPMENTS \par Note: Portfolio companies are listed in the descending order of the total funds \par invested or committed. \par  \par Minuet Ltd is a joint venture between SIHL and an established Thai partner for \par the development of a branded life-style residential and recreational development \par in Bangkok, Thailand. SIHL has a direct 49% interest in the venture, the maximum \par allowable under current regulations, but will be responsible for the design, \par development and execution of the project. \par  \par Update: Advanced discussions with luxury resort operator Amanresorts to build \par the the first Aman club and villas are ongoing. \par  \par The value of Minuet Ltd at 30 September 2010 was US$98.4 million based on an \par independent third party valuation at 30 June 2010. The change in value from \par US$91.7mn at 30 June 2010 is predominantly due to an appreciation in the value \par of the Thai Baht. \par  \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Minor International\f28 \uc2 \f2  Pcl ("MINT") is one of the largest hospitality and \par restaurant companies in the Asia Pacific region with 31 hotels and resorts \par totaling over 3,560 rooms under prominent brands such as the Four Seasons, \par Marriott, Anantara and others in Thailand, Vietnam, Maldives and South Africa. \par MINT also owns and operates 1,123 restaurants under The Pizza Company, \par Swensen's, Sizzler, Dairy Queen, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Burger King\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Thai Express and The Coffee Club. \par  \par Following the restructuring / merger with the Minor Corporation Public Company \par Limited ("MINOR"), MINT's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also include contract manufacturing and an \par international lifestyle consumer brand distribution business in Thailand \par focusing on fashion, cosmetics through retail, wholesale and direct marketing \par channels under brands that include Esprit, Bossini, Red Earth, Bloom, and \par Zwilling Henckels amongst others. \par  \par Update: MINT's revenue and net income increased by 16.6% and 7.9%, respectively, \par in 1H10 year-over-year. The increase was primarily driven by a strong \par performance in 1Q10 from its hotel \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , continued growth of the \par restaurant business and contributions from the consolidation of Minor \par Corporation Pcl ("MINOR") businesses. \par  \par MINT's hotel \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Bangkok were particularly hard hit by the political \par unrest during the second quarter. Businesses located where the demonstrations \par were held effectively closed for two months. The Four Season's hotel in Bangkok \par had an occupancy rate of about 11% in 2Q10. \par  \par The restaurant business continued to perform well driven by stronger same store \par sales and new equity and franchised outlets. Total system sales grew by 8% in \par 1H10 and EBITDA margins increased from 15% to 16%. \par  \par MINT successfully placed a 5-year debenture that was upsized \par from THB2 billion to THB2.5 billion on strong demand. The debenture carried a \par 3.98% coupon and is rated 'A' by TRIS. The debenture was placed to refinance \par existing debt and provide additional liquidity. \par  \par The management of MINT are targeting earnings growth of 20% per annum through \par acquisitions outside of Thailand, additional hotel and restaurant management \par agreements and sales of existing property inventory valued at THB6 billion. \par  \par  \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Parkway Life Real Estate Investment Trust\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("P-REIT") invests in income \par generating healthcare-related properties in the Asia-Pacific region including \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 26-10-10 1039GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the buildings of Parkway's three Singapore hospitals, which are leased back to \par Parkway on long leases. P-REIT is established and managed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Parkway Holdings \par Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  and generates an inflation-linked yield of around 6% based on current \par valuations and historic distributions. \par  \par Update: PREIT's gross revenue and property income increased by 15.3% and 14.5% \par in 1H10 year-over-year to S$37.4 million and S$34.5 million, respectively. \par  \par The strong growth is attributable to the full impact of eight properties \par acquired in Japan in November 2009. \par  \par PREIT announced the acquisition of 11 additional properties in Japan in 2010, \par bringing the total number of Japanese properties to 29. The acquisitions are \par yield accretive. \par  \par Annualised distributions in 1H10 increased to 8.31 Singapore cents from 7.55 \par Singapore cents a share year-on-year or by 10.3%. \par  \par PREIT announced that for the fourth year of lease term for the Singapore \par properties commencing on 23 August 2010, the minimum guaranteed rent will rise \par by 1.73%, based on the inflation linked formula. \par  \par The outlook for PREIT continues to be positive given its substantial debt \par capacity that allows for further yield accretive acquisitions. \par  \par At 30 June 2010, PREIT had gearing of 32.6%, well below the 60% limit allowed by \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore. \par  \par  \par SG Land Co. Ltd ("SG Land") is a joint venture company that owns the leasehold \par rights for two office buildings in downtown Bangkok - SG Tower and Millenia \par Tower. The two buildings in SG Land's portfolio have high occupancy rates and \par offer attractive rental yields. SIHL holds 49.9% of the venture. \par  \par Update: SG Land continues to generate stable performance from rental income on \par its two office towers.  We continue to explore redevelopment and asset \par enhancement approaches in relation to these buildings. \par  \par SG Land was valued at fair value based on an independent third party valuation \par at 30 June 2010. The value of SG Land at at 30 September 2010 was US$16.1 \par million, up from US$14.7mn at 30 June 2010 predominantly due to an appreciation \par in the value of the Thai Baht. \par  \par C Larsen Singapore Pte Limited ("C Larsen") is an importer and distributor of \par high-end U.S. and European furniture brands that include Christian Liaigre, \par Martha Stewart, Barbara Barry, Baker, Herman Miller, Minotti and Thomasville. \par The market served by this business is primarily Thailand, but the intent is to \par grow the business gradually into other parts of Asia. \par  \par  \par Update: C Larsen continues to perform to expectations. The Company continues to \par explore distribution opportunities in other Asian countries, particularly India. \par  \par C Larsen continues to focus on targeting institutional and retail clients. \par  \par AFC Network Pte. Ltd ("AFC") is a 24-hour TV channel broadcasting food and \par lifestyle programming tailored to audiences in the Asia Pacific region. This \par channel began broadcasting in July 2005 and currently airs in Singapore, Hong \par Kong, Malaysia, Indonesia and the Philippines. \par  \par Update: AFC completed a rights issue in June 2010 that was subscribed to by \par existing investors to fund working capital requirements through 2011. The \par Company is presently exploring different options to leverage its competitive \par advantage as the first-mover in the market. \par  \par  \par  \par One Central Residences Macau SIHL invested in four high-end residential \par apartments in a new development in Macau, which was completed ahead of schedule \par in August 2009. \par  \par  \par Update: The Macau property market continues to improve. The development has been \par completed and SIHL took possession of the property units in August 2009. \par  \par SUBSEQUENT EVENTS \par  \par The Company issued 2,059,745 ordinary shares on 21 October 2010, credited as \par fully paid, to the Investment Manager, Symphony Investment Managers Limited, \par increasing the Company's fully paid issued share capital from 342,379,466 \par ordinary shares to 344,439,211 ordinary shares. The shares were issued as part \par of the contractual arrangements with the Investment Manager. If these shares had \par been in issue as at 30 September 2010, SIHL's NAV per share would decrease by \par US$0.0072 per share from US$1.1981 to US$1.1909 per share on that date. \par  \par  \par A more detailed investor update is available upon request from the Company or \par maybe accessed via \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.symphonyasia.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.symphonyasia.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par For further information, please contact: \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sunil Chandiramani\f28 \uc2 \f2  - Symphony Asia Limited (+852 2801 6199) \par  \par The foregoing may contain certain forward looking or forward sounding statements \par with respect to the investments, prospects and/or liquidity of the Company. \par Forward looking statements, by their very nature, involve risk and uncertainty, \par because they relate to circumstances and events that may or may not take place \par in the future due to the numerous factors that could cause actual events to \par differ materially from those implied by any forward looking statements. Neither \par the Company nor its Investment Manager undertake to update any such forward \par looking statements. \par  \par No representation or warranty is made by the Company or its Investment Manager \par as to the accuracy or completeness of the information contained in this document \par and its attachments and no liability will be accepted for any loss whatsoever \par arising in connection with such information. The press releases attached to this \par document were obtained from publicly available sources as at the latest \par practicable time for the preparation of this document. \par  \par This document is for information purposes only and does not constitute an \par invitation or offer to underwrite, subscribe for or otherwise acquire or dispose \par of any securities of the Company in any jurisdiction. All investments are \par subject to risk. Past performance is no guarantee of future returns. \par Shareholders and prospective investors are advised to seek expert legal, \par financial, tax and other professional advice before making any investment \par decisions. \par  \par This document is not for distribution, directly or indirectly, in or into the \par United States or any jurisdiction in which such distribution would be unlawful. \par  \par This announcement is not an offer of securities for sale into the United States. \par The Company's securities have not been, and will not be, registered under the \par United States Securities Act of 1933 and may not be offered or sold in the \par United States absent registration or an exemption from registration. There will \par be no public offer of securities in the United States. \par  \par This information is provided by RNS \par             The company news service from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par    END \par  \par  PFUFEWFSMFSSEDS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 26-10-10 1039GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument RNS0000020101026e6aq000ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
