\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Slips; Support At 29.82\b0\par\par\uc2 1,549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2010\par 03:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB slips to 29.85 vs 29.92-29.93 late yesterday in Asia amid broad USD weakness, after greenback fell sharply overnight vs majors; no sign of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support USD so far. "It's (USD/THB) been correcting for a few days now, so I think it's reversing course. Even though the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  didn't raise interest rates yesterday, the economic data is still strong so it all points to a stronger baht," says Bangkok-based dealer. Pair tipped in 29.82-29.92 range remainder of day. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely lower as USD weakens overnight, EUR sustains gains vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early Asia trade. Pair tipped to consolidate around 6.6500 vs yesterday's 6.6519 close, central parity likely set around 6.6550 vs yesterday's 6.6754. "Today's fixing is likely to return to Tuesday's level after the dollar reversed its previous days' gains overnight," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Adds macroeconomic indicators GDP, CPI due at 0200 GMT likely to affect NDFs more than spot rates, as rate hike already in place to offset expected high inflation for September. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to fall vs 30.930 yesterday, weighed by broad USD weakness, expected gains in Taiwan shares, says foreign bank trader. Tips pair in 30.700-30.850 band. Still, "the pace of the pair's decline won't be so brisk," as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  determined to curb TWD strength, he says. Reckons players will keep close eye on USD/CNY as Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been key market driver in recent days. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] If China economic data around 0200 GMT proves stronger than expected, players will be set to sell safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like USD, JPY, says Keiichi Iguchi, senior dealer at Resona Bank. Says EUR/USD may hit 1.4150 in that scenario (last at 1.3943) but further gains unlikely as investors cautious about positioning either way before G-20 finance ministers meeting; adds USD sales will likely outweigh those for JPY, taking USD/JPY to fresh 15-year low of 80.80, where pair likely supported by Japan importers. Now at 81.08. Economists polled by Dow Jones expect China 3Q GDP +9.5% on-year vs 10.3% rise in previous quarter, September CPI +3.6% on-year vs +3.5% in August, industrial output +13.6% vs +13.9%. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to trade in range with yields moving 2-3 bps either way as investors remain mostly on sidelines looking for fresh cues on outlook for the global economy, US Fed moves, says trader with foreign bank. "Yields are low, but investors don't want to buy or sell," trader says. Yesterday, 2-year yields rose 1 bp to close 0.41%, while 10-year gained 2 bps to close 1.21%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. Treasurys likely to extend gains in coming weeks as investors continue to expect more Fed easing, with "overall bullish tone" possibly providing some support for Asia rates today, says Wee-Khoon Chong, rates strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . While 10-year Treasurys flat late NY, 2-year yield down 1.2 bps at 0.351%, while 5-year yield down 0.6 bps at 1.097% after earlier touching record low of 1.069%. While any further strength in global equities could weigh on demand for safe-haven Treasurys, house continues "to see USTs outperforming up to the FOMC 3 November meeting, more so in the 5-10-year sector." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] Growing belief in markets that QE2 will be a classic "sell on fact" event for equities is misplaced, says Southern Cross Equities director Charlie Aitken. "In my opinion the confirmation of the Fed diluting all holders of US dollars again by printing money will see further outflows from US dollars into the high growth \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the world, and those quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the world that can't be diluted, currently known as commodities." Says Fed can't risk "understimulating" stalled U.S. economy. "All you are seeing is an acknowledgement from global investors and asset allocators that the highly indebted industrialised countries are going to have ultra-low interest rates, ultra-low growth, ultra-low inflation and high unemployment for an extended period...yet in Asia (ex-Japan), the Middle East and South America, growth rates will be double-digit and increasingly driven by domestic consumption," says Aitken. Adds that China's surprise interest rate hike was "just another buying opportunity in new world-leveraged assets." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last down 0.6% at 1860.13 as foreigners selling in futures, spot markets, with strong upside momentum lacking in techs in particular; "investors, disappointed by \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' weaker-than-expected 3Q guidance, seem to find no reason to buy technology stocks as major IT companies such as Hynix and LG Display have yet to come up with their quarterly results," says Lee Kyoung-min at \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2 . Tips Kospi to trade below 1880 today. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.1% at KRW744,000, LG Display (034220.SE) off 1.1% at KRW39,800. \uc2 Samsung SDI\uc2  (006400.SE) flat at KRW148,500. Among financial stocks, \uc2 Hana Financial Group\uc2  (086790.SE) showing biggest decline, falling 5.3% at KRW33,650; this after person familiar with situation tells Dow Jones Singapore's \uc2 Temasek Holdings\uc2  sold entire 9.6% stake in \uc2 Hana Financial\uc2  at 6% discount to yesterday's closing price. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to extend yesterday's gains; Taiwan International Securities' analyst Andrew Teng reckons tech stocks will continue to lead broader market higher, Taiex likely to hit 8200 today, while strong support at 8000. Benchmark index ended +1.0% at 8124.62 Wednesday. "About 64% of yesterday's trading volume were on tech stocks, that's a good sign, which suggests the momentum of the current rebound is quite strong." Acer (2353.TW) likely to climb further on record-high 3Q net profit, stock ended +2.1% at NT$80.50 yesterday. Heavyweights likely to continue to recoup recent losses; at yesterday's close, Hon Hai (2317.TW) +1.8% at NT$114.00, TSMC (2330.TW) +0.5% at NT$61.00. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped to move lower in line with broad USD weakness amid overnight gains of U.S. equities. Pair likely to head towards 43.15 support from 43.37 late Wednesday. "Worries triggered by the China rate hike have subsided and appetite for riskier assets has resumed," notes local bank trader. Notes, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support pair at 43.15 level. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares likely to rise after Wall Street's broad gains Wednesday on positive earnings from airlines, upbeat outlook from financials. "We had a strong bounce in the U.S. last night, so the STI is likely to follow suit today, though the magnitude of gains may not be as big as we didn't fall as much yesterday. So perhaps we'll just see a small rebound," says strategist at foreign brokerage. Adds, all eyes on September economic data from China today, most attention likely on CPI figures to gauge timing, likelihood of further PBOC rate hikes. \uc2 Mapletree Industrial Trust\uc2  (ME8U.SG) debuts in afternoon trade today at 0600 GMT, second large Singapore IPO this week; "the unit is likely to rise in line with recent IPOs, it could even outperform GLP (MC0.SG) as the offer was heavily in demand," strategist says. \uc2 CapitaCommercial Trust\uc2  (C61U.SG) also in focus after 7.6% on-year rise in DPU for 3Q. STI closed down 0.4% at 3,179.15 Wednesday; may test recent peak around 3220, which highest since May 2008. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei off 0.3% at 9349.59, nearing fresh 2-week low; Nomura Securities notes People's Bank of China's decision to raise interest rates for both deposits and lending rates on October 19 (raising 1-year benchmark lending rate by 25bp to 5.56%), while a surprise, was in-line with house's expectations. Says move likely prompted by 1) rise in equity prices, 2) increased activity in property market, 3) robust economic growth despite the structural adjustment policy, and 4) relatively high CPI inflation. Adds, China will likely raise interest rates 4 times next year in its move to normalize monetary policy. Japan's equities markets suffered in wake of China's first hike in almost 3 years, with Nikkei closing down 1.7%. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com) [ 10-20-10 2100ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101021e6al0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
