\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/INR Off Lows As Importers Buy, Stocks Fall\b0\par\par\uc2 1,563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 11:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0942 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR off lows as oil-importers buy, say traders; add, weak local stocks also lending support. Pair last at 44.18 vs 44.09 intraday low, 44.52 late Wednesday. "There's been some talk about this potentially being an RBI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but this could just as easily be a bunch of importers buying, particularly since we've received heavy importer orders too," says trader with private bank. Trader with state-run bank typically tapped for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  related purchases says bank hasn't received orders from RBI, reckons purchases related to routine importer buying. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai authorities considering additional measures to curb hot money inflows as withholding tax imposed on gain from foreign investment of Thai bonds unlikely to stem THB's rise. THB hovering around 13-year highs, up over 10% YTD, making it one of strongest performers in Asia. But most don't expect draconian moves in near future. Tax on short-term capital inflows would require time-consuming legislation; reimposition of full, partial capital controls would hurt investment climate while such controls proved ineffective when imposed in 2006. BOT could continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but act has limitations, given \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's swollen reserves, increasing cost of sterilization. "The crux is that it is difficult to fight the tide of foreign inflows into emerging markets. The authorities can impose more control measures but they can, at best, only slow the appreciation pressure on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  strategist Pin Ru Tan." With weaker USD ahead, government should seek longer-term solutions that involve ways to narrow current account surplus rather than looking for quick fix on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front, says Pornthep Jubandhu, manager of macroeconomic analysis at \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2 's Economics Intelligence Center. See: =FOCUS:Thai Bond Tax Won't Stop Baht Rise;No Harsh Move In Sight (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF appears to be carving out a top against its 21-week moving average near 1.35 says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . If this scenario is correct then the cross should start to slip toward 1.3265 and on a break here 1.3165 and 1.3070. Movement above 1.35 would indicate that that bias is incorrect, says the bank. EUR/CHF now at 1.3370. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds higher in moderate trade. Local players accumulate bonds while trading activity by offshore players fades since Thailand reinstates 15% withholding tax on bond interest, capital gains for foreigners. "It may take a while (for offshore players) to return. However, the bearish view on the U.S. dollar will eventually spur capital inflows to the bond market," says local dealer. Adds, some investors starting to think \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  may stand pat at Oct. 20 meeting after mounting pressure on strong baht. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.45%/2.42% vs 2.52%/2.49% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.64%/2.58% vs 2.67%/2.62%, June 2019 at 2.85%/2.82% vs 2.88%/2.87%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 14 may trade in range of 100-110 bps in coming sessions before earning reports from major Japan firms due to start late next week, says Yasuhiro Matsumoto, senior analyst at \uc2 Shinsei Securities\uc2 ; says players may price in somewhat negative outcome beforehand mainly due to recent JPY strength. Says "if earnings miss market expectations significantly, (benchmark index) will likely widen." iTraxx trades at 100, 101 bps, little changed from Wednesday's indication at 99/102 bps. Single-name CDS deals traded today include \uc2 JFE Steel\uc2  at 60 bps, unchanged from Sept. 14, Komatsu (6301.TO) at 40 bps, unchanged from Oct. 4, \uc2 Marubeni Corp\uc2 . (8002.TO) at 100 bps vs 117 bps on Sept. 9, \uc2 Mitsubishi Corp\uc2 . (8058.TO) at 45 bps vs 48 bps on Sept. 13, \uc2 Orix Corp\uc2 . (8591.TO) at 230 bps vs 240 bps on Oct. 6, \uc2 Sumitomo Realty\uc2  (8830.TO) at 110 bps vs 135 bps on Aug. 3, \uc2 Kawasaki Heavy Industries\uc2  (7012.TO) at 100 bps vs 105 bps on Sept. 13, Acom (8572.TO) at 520, 540, 550 bps, vs 550, 530 bps yesterday, Promise (8574.TO) at 885 bps vs 840 bps on Oct. 7, Promise's 3.75-year at 900 bps. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY momentarily trades below 81.00 for the first time since April 1995. The spot is now over 200 sen lower from when the MOF sanctioned \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on September 15th and the BOJ spent over Y2Tln to keep USD/JPY from falling below 83.00. The spot now trades at 81.03 from a low of 80.97. Looking at the charts \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones says next support comes in at 80.77 and 80.40 and then the all-time low of 79.90. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0919 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore shibor-based IRS up, playing catch-up with repo-based IRS. 1-year shibor-based IRS up 3 bps at 2.81%, 5-year +5 bps at 3.44%. "I heard that several big Chinese banks are paying shibor-based IRS as they think shibor-repo spread has been too narrow," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. 5-year repo-based IRS up around 20 bps since early September on inflation concerns. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] Concerned by KRW's 15% surge vs USD since May, Korean government appears to be weighing plans to tax foreigners' purchases of local bonds, similar to plan just passed in Thailand. But Korea should avoid the temptation, since tax would do little to discourage hot-money flows driving KRW's rise, while hurting market confidence in government and harming Seoul's bid to join a widely followed global bond index. Tax option raised by a South Korean lawmaker Monday, with FSC chairman replying that though FSC isn't in charge of tax issues, it "will discuss the possibility with related ministries." But even with tax, Korean bond yields are likely to remain attractive to foreign investors, while strength of local economy also helps keep KRW on investors' radars. SEE: "MONEY TALKS: South Korea Should Not Copy Thais' Bond Tax Move" (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com, kanga.kong@dowjones.com )\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end steady at 1,496.38, after touching fresh 33-month high of 1,503.82; "with (2011) budget announcement due tomorrow, investors aren't too keen on taking aggressive positions," says dealer. Still, OSK Research says near-term outlook remains bullish; "the room is still open for additional gains before the FBM KLCI reaches its overbought territory." Tips initial support at 1,497.6, with psychological 1,500 still acting as resistance. Market breadth negative with 455 decliners vs 348 gainers. Among decliners, \uc2 Genting Bhd\uc2  (3182.KU) down 2.5% at MYR10.20, CIMB Group (1023.KU) down 0.9% at MYR8.00. Among gainers, AirAsia (5099.KU) +7.8% at MYR2.35, Manulife (1058.KU) +12% at MYR3.19 after major shareholder makes offer to buy out remaining stake in insurer. Plantation stocks higher with \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +2.2% at MYR19, supported by higher CPO prices. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0914 G