\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Bond Tax Won't Stop THB Rise - FOCUS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 11:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai authorities considering additional measures to curb hot money inflows as withholding tax imposed on gain from foreign investment of Thai bonds unlikely to stem THB's rise. THB hovering around 13-year highs, up over 10% YTD, making it one of strongest performers in Asia. But most don't expect draconian moves in near future. Tax on short-term capital inflows would require time-consuming legislation; reimposition of full, partial capital controls would hurt investment climate while such controls proved ineffective when imposed in 2006. BOT could continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but act has limitations, given \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's swollen reserves, increasing cost of sterilization. "The crux is that it is difficult to fight the tide of foreign inflows into emerging markets. The authorities can impose more control measures but they can, at best, only slow the appreciation pressure on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  strategist Pin Ru Tan." With weaker USD ahead, government should seek longer-term solutions that involve ways to narrow current account surplus rather than looking for quick fix on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front, says Pornthep Jubandhu, manager of macroeconomic analysis at \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2 's Economics Intelligence Center. See: =FOCUS:Thai Bond Tax Won't Stop Baht Rise;No Harsh Move In Sight (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF appears to be carving out a top against its 21-week moving average near 1.35 says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . If this scenario is correct then the cross should start to slip toward 1.3265 and on a break here 1.3165 and 1.3070. Movement above 1.35 would indicate that that bias is incorrect, says the bank. EUR/CHF now at 1.3370. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds higher in moderate trade. Local players accumulate bonds while trading activity by offshore players fades since Thailand reinstates 15% withholding tax on bond interest, capital gains for foreigners. "It may take a while (for offshore players) to return. However, the bearish view on the U.S. dollar will eventually spur capital inflows to the bond market," says local dealer. Adds, some investors starting to think \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  may stand pat at Oct. 20 meeting after mounting pressure on strong baht. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.45%/2.42% vs 2.52%/2.49% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.64%/2.58% vs 2.67%/2.62%, June 2019 at 2.85%/2.82% vs 2.88%/2.87%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 14 may trade in range of 100-110 bps in coming sessions before earning reports from major Japan firms due to start late next week, says Yasuhiro Matsumoto, senior analyst at \uc2 Shinsei Securities\uc2 ; says players may price in somewhat negative outcome beforehand mainly due to recent JPY strength. Says "if earnings miss market expectations significantly, (benchmark index) will likely widen." iTraxx trades at 100, 101 bps, little changed from Wednesday's indication at 99/102 bps. Single-name CDS deals traded today include \uc2 JFE Steel\uc2  at 60 bps, unchanged from Sept. 14, Komatsu (6301.TO) at 40 bps, unchanged from Oct. 4, \uc2 Marubeni Corp\uc2 . (8002.TO) at 100 bps vs 117 bps on Sept. 9, \uc2 Mitsubishi Corp\uc2 . (8058.TO) at 45 bps vs 48 bps on Sept. 13, \uc2 Orix Corp\uc2 . (8591.TO) at 2