\par\par \b \uc2 Thai PM: No Plan To Move To Exchange-Rate Targeting\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jenny Paris   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 00:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Thailand won't target a specific exchange rate for the baht even as the strength of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is trading at a 13-year high, is hurting the country's vital export industry, Prime Minister Abhisit Vejjajiva said in an interview Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Abhisit said that the government instead would focus on helping sectors in the economy that are affected by the strong baht by providing better hedging tools for them through local policy banks.\par \par \uc2 "We'd rather target those affected sectors--maybe provide some help in terms of their ability to hedge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks better rather than trying to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market," Abhisit said in a joint interview with The Wall Street Journal and Dow Jones Newswires. "We don't think we should move to some kind of exchange-rate targeting."\par \par \uc2 Abhisit reiterated that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is meant to stem excess \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility rather than achieve a particular level for the baht. The dollar fell below THB30 Tuesday for the first time since July 1997.\par \par \uc2 Abhisit said Thailand's robust economy, including gross domestic product growth of around 8% this year, export growth of around 30% and a healthy current account surplus, point to a stronger baht even as Thai exporters are complaining about their ability to maintain their competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 Referring to global efforts to tame the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as countries around the world struggle to hold their soaring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down in what has come to be termed as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, Abhisit said that any realignment of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels must look at economic fundamentals first. He called on the Group of 20 world's largest economies, or G-20, to reach some kind of coordination on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of China's move toward a more flexible exchange rate for the yuan, he said the movement in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June when China dropped the yuan's almost two-year de facto peg to the dollar hasn't been as great as expected.\par \par \uc2 "People may have exaggerated how much can be achieved" through a more flexible yuan, he said without elaborating.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the domestic political scene, Abhisit said that recent violence wasn't conducive to a climate of reconciliation that must be achieved before a national election is held.\par \par \uc2 Thai investigators Wednesday said they found evidence of additional bomb-making materials at the site of a blast that killed four people at a suburban Bangkok apartment building a day earlier, heightening fears that opposition Red Shirt activists are stockpiling explosives to stage violent attacks.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's government had just extended for three months on Tuesday a long-running state of emergency banning political activity in the capital, citing the continuing threat from bombing campaigns. A series of explosions have rattled Bangkok since the military put down a mammoth antigovernment demonstration in the city in May.\par \par \uc2 Some 91 people were killed in clashes between security forces and the Red Shirt protesters, who were demanding new elections and campaigning for the return of ousted populist leader Thaksin Shinawatra. Thaksin was overthrown in a military coup four years ago and is now living overseas to escape imprisonment on a corruption conviction--a charge he denies and calls politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 Abhisit's military-backed government, whose term ends at the end of 2011, has so far resisted calls for early elections. Abhisit didn't rule out the possibility of the elections taking place before the end of the current term, but he stressed that political stability must be achieved first.\par \par \uc2 "A democratic election has to be free and peaceful," he said. "I don't want to win in a bloody election. I'd rather lose in a peaceful one."\par \par \uc2 Turning to the controversial case of alleged Russian arms dealer Viktor Bout, whose extradition to the U.S. has created tensions between the three countries, the prime minister said Thailand hopes officials in the U.S. and Russia can come to an agreement.\par \par \uc2 "We don't want to upset our relations with either the U.S. or Russia," Abhisit said.\par \par \uc2 "We'd rather the two parties will talk to each other rather than make demands from us."\par \par \uc2 -By Jenny Paris, Dow Jones Newswires; jenny.paris@dowjones.com [ 10-06-10 1805ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101006e6a6000w5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
