\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 10:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB BINI SMAGHI: PROPOSED LIQUIDITY RULES TO CAUSE 'CLIFF EFFECT' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Proposed new liquidity regulation will lead to a "cliff effect," causing temporary challenges to the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which sets interest rates in the euro zone, a key member of the ECB's Executive Board said Wednesday. (published 0750 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH SEP CONSUMER CONFIDENCE IMPROVES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French households grew more confident in September from August, as they believed France's standards of living were improving along with their personal financial situation, while concerns regarding the labor market decreased, according to data released Wednesday by the French national statistics office Insee. (published 0652 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FSA CHIEF URGES FINANCIAL TRANSACTION TAX CONSIDERATION- REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of U.K. regulator Financial Services Authority Adair Turner says European Union finance ministers should look closely at a financial transaction tax, a concept rejected by U.K. Treasurer George Osborne, the daily Handelsblatt reports in Wednesday's edition. (published 0630 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU REHN: IRISH GOVERNMENT MUST SPECIFY FUTURE BUDGET CUTS -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Irish government should identify now the cuts it will make over the next few years in order to meet its target of cutting the budget deficit to 3% of gross domestic product by 2014, European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Olli Rehn said in an interview published Wednesday. (published 0738 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITALY BUSINESS CONFIDENCE FALLS, FIRST TIME IN 18 MONTHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian business confidence in September fell for the first time in 18 months on weaker views on current demand, current production and investment goods, data showed Wednesday. (published 0748 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN TANKAN SHOWS SEPTEMBER IMPROVEMENT, BUT OUTLOOK DARKENS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sentiment among Japan's big manufacturers picked up for the sixth straight quarter at a faster pace than expected, the Bank of Japan's September tankan survey showed Wednesday, but companies are turning more cautious in their outlook for the coming quarter due to the surging yen and increased uncertainty over the global economy. (published 0212 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HSBC CHINA SEPTEMBER PMI RISES TO FIVE-MONTH HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, rose to a five-month high in September, likely reassuring markets China isn't facing a rapid slowdown, but sharp increases in the input and output price subindexes may renew concerns about a buildup of inflationary pressures. (published 0337 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN SEP HCPI +2% ON YEAR VS +1.8% IN AUG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spanish consumer price inflation accelerated in September, partly the result of higher fuel and lubricants prices, Spain's National Statistics Institute, or INE, said in a statement Wednesday. (published 0711 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN FINANCE MINISTER: WILL MAKE FURTHER BUDGET CUTS IF NEEDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain's government is willing to make further cuts in spending if that is necessary, although that isn't currently the case, Finance Minister Elena Salgado said in an interview broadcast Wednesday. (published 0634 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RESERVE BANK OF AUSTRALIA'S BROADBENT EXPECTS HIGHER AUSTRALIA GROWTH IN 2011 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy will see stronger growth in 2011 spurred on by China, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  board member \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jillian Broadbent\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wednesday ahead of a closely watched rate decision next week when the bank is expected to tighten policy in an effort to manage the country's surging economy. (published 0513 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN HOUSING FACES FITCH STRESS TEST AMID BUBBLE WORRY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's soaring property prices will come under fresh international scrutiny with Fitch Ratings Inc. announcing Wednesday plans to stress test the country's mortgage market after receiving a growing number of inquiries about its sustainability. (published 0741 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA REVENUE SECRETARY: TO MEET TAX REVENUE AIM OF INR7.45 TLN THIS FY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's federal government expects to meet its tax revenue target of INR7.45 trillion for the current fiscal year that began April 1, Revenue Secretary Sunil Mitra said Wednesday. (published 0630 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SWEDISH CONSUMER CONFIDENCE AT NEW 10-YEAR HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Swedish consumers remain optimistic about the economy, with confidence rising to a new 10-year high in September, a survey showed Wednesday. (published 0749 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA: SHOULD BALANCE INFLATION, EXTERNAL RISKS WHEN OPERATING MACRO POLICIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea authorities should weigh the risk of faster inflation and a softer recovery in the global economy when implementing macroeconomic policies, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Wednesday. (published 0500 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA AUGUST CURRENT ACCOUNT SURPLUS FALLS; TO BEAT 2010 TARGET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea ran its smallest current-account surplus in four months in August as trade gains shrank due to summer holidays, but the country is expected to chalk up a surplus for the full year that will easily surpass the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's $21 billion estimate. (published 0207 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NZ EXPORTS WEAKEN, REDUCING SUPPORT FOR RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's goods trade balance recorded a deficit in August after exports came in weaker than expected, stoking concerns over the strength of the country's economic recovery. (published 0235 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SINGAPORE GIC SEES BETTER OPPORTUNITIES IN REAL ESTATE, PRIVATE EQUITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government of Singapore Investment Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., or GIC, said Wednesday it sees more rewarding opportunities in real estate and private equity, and that it will continue investing in publicly listed equities as part of its emerging market strategy. (published 0548 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S REGIONAL DEVELOPMENT MINISTER SUPPORTS CHINESE INVESTMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Regional Development Minister Simon Crean Wednesday said he is a strong believer in foreign investment to help develop the country, given the historical lack of large domestic savings to fund investments and infrastructure development. (published 0557 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TURKEY FINANCE MINISTER: CRUCIAL TO HIT BUDGET TARGETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is "crucial and critical" for the Turkish government to hit its budget targets as Ankara cannot risk its fiscal credibility, Finance Minister Mehmet Simsek said Wednesday. (published 0659 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THAI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : BAHT STRENGTH STILL IN LINE WITH REGIONAL \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Thailand Assistant Governor Suchada Kirakul said Wednesday that the baht's strength is still in line with that of regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0541 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: ROUGHED UP BY THE FED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar faces a rough time ahead. The simple issue is this: more quantitative easing in the U.S. means lower U.S. yields and a less attractive dollar as far as international investors are concerned. (published 0715 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: MORE UPSIDE FOR BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Renewed bull pressure is expected on Tuesday's high at 131.99 as the focus swings to the Sep. 8 lower high at 132.14. The upside gap at 130.25 underpins strength, and room exists for gains to the 132.30 area where congested projected resistance lies, protecting the Aug. 31 contract high at 133.29. Corrective weakness will attract support while above 131.25, but backup support lies at 130.75. (published 0703 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR/USD HEADING HIGHER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: The strong upward bias is set to challenge the 1.3600 level, opening up the April 12 lower high at 1.3692. Tuesday's sustained break above 1.3507 keeps bulls in control of the near-term, setting their sights on projected resistance at 1.3702. Corrective weakness will attract support while above 1.3495, and only below there would concern bulls. (published 0613 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN CENTRAL BANKS KEEP UP \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS DOLLAR WEAKENS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The central banks of South Korea, Singapore, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Indonesia were suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange markets Wednesday to contain gains in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but analysts say the continued weakness of the U.S. dollar means Asian units should extend their rallies. (published 0555 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: TAIWAN LIKELY TO RAISE POLICY RATE BY 12.5 BPS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely raise its key policy interest rate by 12.5 basis points at its quarterly meeting Thursday as the local economy continues to recover despite a slowdown in global growth, with prices in the property market soaring. (published 0341 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: TAIWAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LIKELY TO PAUSE AFTER THU \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely expected to hike policy interest rates by 12.5 basis points Thursday -- but after that it will most likely hold steady until at least the end of this year. (published 0453 GMT) [ 29-09-10 0800GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100929e69t000n0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
