\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,215 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 09:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH SEP CONSUMER CONFIDENCE IMPROVES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French households grew more confident in September from August, as they believed France's standards of living were improving along with their personal financial situation, while concerns regarding the labor market decreased, according to data released Wednesday by the French national statistics office Insee. (published 0652 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FSA CHIEF URGES FINANCIAL TRANSACTION TAX CONSIDERATION- REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of U.K. regulator Financial Services Authority Adair Turner says European Union finance ministers should look closely at a financial transaction tax, a concept rejected by U.K. Treasurer George Osborne, the daily Handelsblatt reports in Wednesday's edition. (published 0630 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN TANKAN SHOWS SEPTEMBER IMPROVEMENT, BUT OUTLOOK DARKENS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sentiment among Japan's big manufacturers picked up for the sixth straight quarter at a faster pace than expected, the Bank of Japan's September tankan survey showed Wednesday, but companies are turning more cautious in their outlook for the coming quarter due to the surging yen and increased uncertainty over the global economy. (published 0212 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HSBC CHINA SEPTEMBER PMI RISES TO FIVE-MONTH HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, rose to a five-month high in September, likely reassuring markets China isn't facing a rapid slowdown, but sharp increases in the input and output price subindexes may renew concerns about a buildup of inflationary pressures. (published 0337 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN FINANCE MINISTER: WILL MAKE FURTHER BUDGET CUTS IF NEEDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain's government is willing to make further cuts in spending if that is necessary, although that isn't currently the case, Finance Minister Elena Salgado said in an interview broadcast Wednesday. (published 0634 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RESERVE BANK OF AUSTRALIA'S BROADBENT EXPECTS HIGHER AUSTRALIA GROWTH IN 2011 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy will see stronger growth in 2011 spurred on by China, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  board member \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jillian Broadbent\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wednesday ahead of a closely watched rate decision next week when the bank is expected to tighten policy in an effort to manage the country's surging economy. (published 0513 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA REVENUE SECRETARY: TO MEET TAX REVENUE AIM OF INR7.45 TLN THIS FY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's federal government expects to meet its tax revenue target of INR7.45 trillion for the current fiscal year that began April 1, Revenue Secretary Sunil Mitra said Wednesday. (published 0630 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA: SHOULD BALANCE INFLATION, EXTERNAL RISKS WHEN OPERATING MACRO POLICIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea authorities should weigh the risk of faster inflation and a softer recovery in the global economy when implementing macroeconomic policies, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Wednesday. (published 0500 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA AUGUST CURRENT ACCOUNT SURPLUS FALLS; TO BEAT 2010 TARGET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea ran its smallest current-account surplus in four months in August as trade gains shrank due to summer holidays, but the country is expected to chalk up a surplus for the full year that will easily surpass the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's $21 billion estimate. (published 0207 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NZ EXPORTS WEAKEN, REDUCING SUPPORT FOR RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's goods trade balance recorded a deficit in August after exports came in weaker than expected, stoking concerns over the strength of the country's economic recovery. (published 0235 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SINGAPORE GIC SEES BETTER OPPORTUNITIES IN REAL ESTATE, PRIVATE EQUITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government of Singapore Investment Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., or GIC, said Wednesday it sees more rewarding opportunities in real estate and private equity, and that it will continue investing in publicly listed equities as part of its emerging market strategy. (published 0548 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S REGIONAL DEVELOPMENT MINISTER SUPPORTS CHINESE INVESTMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Regional Development Minister Simon Crean Wednesday said he is a strong believer in foreign investment to help develop the country, given the historical lack of large domestic savings to fund investments and infrastructure development. (published 0557 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CARLOS SLIM SAYS AUSTRALIA, ASIA PACIFIC ARE VERY IMPORTANT RIGHT NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Carlos Slim, the world's richest man, said Wednesday that Australia and the Asia-Pacific region are very important but drew back from plans to make any investments in the region. (published 0503 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TURKEY FINANCE MINISTER: CRUCIAL TO HIT BUDGET TARGETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is "crucial and critical" for the Turkish government to hit its budget targets as Ankara cannot risk its fiscal credibility, Finance Minister Mehmet Simsek said Wednesday. (published 0659 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THAI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : BAHT STRENGTH STILL IN LINE WITH REGIONAL \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Thailand Assistant Governor Suchada Kirakul said Wednesday that the baht's strength is still in line with that of regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0541 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THAI FINANCE MINISTRY EXPECTS GREATER FUND INFLOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Wednesday it expects more funds to enter the country's financial markets, adding that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2 's recent comments suggesting the country's policy rate will rise have intensified inflows. (published 0654 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IMF'S STRAUSS-KAHN: OPTIMISTIC ON GLOBAL RECOVERY, DOUBLE-DIP RISK LOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of the International Monetary Fund is optimistic that the global recovery will continue despite recent sluggishness in rich countries, and he said the risk of another downturn is unlikely. (published 2310 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FED'S LOCKHART: ECONOMY SHOULD SEE GRADUAL IMPROVEMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The economy should see gradual improvement over the coming years, Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta President Dennis Lockhart said Tuesday. (published 2337 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FED'S WARSH: WANTS US DOLLAR TO REMAIN 'RELIABLE,' STABLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A top U.S. Federal Reserve official Tuesday said there are advantages in having a stable and "reliable" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the U.S. dollar, cautioning against devaluing a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to boost an economy. (published 2249 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR/USD HEADING HIGHER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: The strong upward bias is set to challenge the 1.3600 level, opening up the April 12 lower high at 1.3692. Tuesday's sustained break above 1.3507 keeps bulls in control of the near-term, setting their sights on projected resistance at 1.3702. Corrective weakness will attract support while above 1.3495, and only below there would concern bulls. (published 0613 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN CENTRAL BANKS KEEP UP \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS DOLLAR WEAKENS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The central banks of South Korea, Singapore, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Indonesia were suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange markets Wednesday to contain gains in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but analysts say the continued weakness of the U.S. dollar means Asian units should extend their rallies. (published 0555 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: TAIWAN LIKELY TO RAISE POLICY RATE BY 12.5 BPS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely raise its key policy interest rate by 12.5 basis points at its quarterly meeting Thursday as the local economy continues to recover despite a slowdown in global growth, with prices in the property market soaring. (published 0341 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: TAIWAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LIKELY TO PAUSE AFTER THU \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely expected to hike policy interest rates by 12.5 basis points Thursday -- but after that it will most likely hold steady until at least the end of this year. (published 0453 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: NATIONS RACE TO WEAKEN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , SPARKING TRADE WAR FEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tensions are growing in the global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets as political rhetoric heats up and countries battle to protect their exporters, raising concerns about potentially damaging trade wars. (published 2233 GMT) [ 29-09-10 0700GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100929e69t000in\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
