\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Shares Off 0.8%; Support Tipped At 908-905\b0\par\par\uc2 1,615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 05:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0339 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares off 0.8% at 914.98 on lingering concerns BOT may impose stronger measures to stem THB's rise. Foreigners' net selling yesterday also weighing market sentiment. Energy, petrochemical plays falling on concerns residents in Rayong will stage protest against recent court's ruling allowing 74 of 76 suspended projects at Map Ta Phut to proceed, while telecom stocks also under pressure after court set hearing for today on legal challenge to 3G mobile license auction, with state-owned \uc2 CAT Telecom\uc2  seeking injunction. "A spate of negative factors is hitting the market," says Capital Nomura Securities strategist Chai Jirasevenupraphund. Support tipped at 908-905, resistance at 921. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) off 1.1% at THB279, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) flat at THB119, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) down 1.9% at THB92.75, \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  off 1.3% at THB96, \uc2 Total Access Communication\uc2  (DTAC.TH) off 1.1% at THB45.25. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0332 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB higher at 30.82-30.84 vs 30.73-30.74 early, boosted by news Japan's MOF has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on JPY, which prompts suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected to have bought USD to push pair to intrasession high of 30.85, dealers say. "At first we thought the dollar/baht would be rangebound and biased downward. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  move by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and subsequently the suspected BOT \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  changed the direction," says one dealer. Some players jump on bandwagon covering shorts, another dealer says. Pair tipped in 30.75-30.90 range. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian skilled vacancies data, +0.1% on month in September vs 0.3% fall in August, reflect "super strength" of labor market, \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  analyst Roland Randall says. Adds data - persisting near post financial crisis highs - consistent with continued strong employment growth, pushing unemployment rate down from 5.1% currently. Still, momentum in labor market "does appear to be slowing", so urgency for RBA to tighten rates sooner rather than later to combat labor market capacity constraints has eased recently. Mining state of Western Australia continues to account for an "outsized share" of job openings - 34% nationally, dominating demand for skilled tradespeople. (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0325 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi turns lower, last down 0.1% at 1813.61, as news of Japan MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY weighing on techs, capping gains in autos due to concerns over price competitiveness in export markets; "in particular, a weak yen is putting a pressure on IT parts shares such as Samsung SDI and Cheil Industries, while \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  won't rise further," says Kim Jung-hoon at \uc2 Korea Investment & Securities\uc2 . Tips Kospi in 1810-1820 band. Adds domestic institutions' net selling of stocks worth KRW127 billion also weighs. Samsung SDI (006400.SE) down 6.1% at KRW154,500, Cheil Industries (001300.SE) off 7.5% at KRW99,000; among auto stocks, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +1.0% at KRW158,500, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +2.0% at KRW35,050. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open tad lower tracking overnight EUR gains vs USD, but news of Japan MOF's JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may limit fall, says dealer with foreign bank; pair likely to open at 46.40 vs 46.46 late yesterday. "While euro was strong overnight, the dollar has clawed back after Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, the rupee is unlikely to be affected as it is already a laggard amongst Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the RBI has stayed away from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," says dealer. Notes global stock markets also broadly flat which might cap gains in local unit. Tips pair in 46.30-46.60 band in session. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR16.51 billion worth of stocks yesterday, 1-month NDFs quoting at 46.51/46.54. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR at 3.1150 vs 3.1040 late yesterday, off earlier low of 3.1000, tracking rebound in USD vs other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Japan's MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market; dealers say news of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  coupled with suspected USD buying by Bank Negara at 3.1000 this week prompting market participants to take profits on MYR's recent appreciation. "Because of the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks in the region, players see little room for further upside for the ringgit in the near-term and are just content to take profits for now," dealer says; tips USD/MYR to remain in 3.1000-3.1200 range near-term. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open tad higher by about 15-30 points helped by gains in most Asian markets and as mood remains upbeat after index managed to hold its intraday gains to end up for 6th-consecutive session yesterday, says local dealer. Adds, strong liquidity on back of sustained buying by foreign funds to further help trade. Overseas funds bought shares worth provisional INR16.51 billion Tuesday vs INR26.48 billion Monday. However, believes upside should be capped at 19,470--says technically markets look overbought--up over 6% in last 6 session and traders could start consolidating positions. Stretched valuations in most index stocks after recent rally, caution before RBI rate-setting meeting tomorrow to also cap upside. Sensex ended +0.7% at 19,346.96 Tuesday. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures turn lower following news of Japan MOF's JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which drags down KRW. "Following the yen's slip on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expectations of a weaker won are spurring concerns that foreign buying in won-based bonds may falter," says \uc2 SK Securities\uc2  analyst Yum Sang-hoon. Expects local bonds to show lackluster performance on unexpected KRW weakness. But adds "how effective Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be in keeping the yen weak remains in question, so I don't expect the negative impact on local bonds to last long." 3-year yield +2 bps at 3.41%, September futures down 12 ticks at 112.68 vs earlier 112.98 high. USD/KRW now up at 1,164.5 vs 1,161.7 yesterday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds likely to open higher tracking lower U.S. Treasury yields but gains likely limited ahead of rate setting meeting Thursday, says dealer with private bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond likely to open in 99.05-99.15 band vs 99.01 at Tuesday's close. Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee Tuesday said despite lower August inflation there was no room for complacency and government will work towards lowering it further, use fiscal, monetary instruments as needed; comments will cement market expectation of a 25 basis points hike in rates by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "A quarter percentage hike is almost factored in. If the RBI decides to pause, the 10-year yield may rally to 7.90% (closed at 7.95%)," says dealer. Tips 2020 bond to be in 98.95-99.40 range in session. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares sharply higher as local bourse plays catch-up after week-long holiday, dealers say; main index +3.2% at fresh all-time high of 3323.86. Market breadth positive with 108 gainers vs 17 decliners. "Foreigners continue to buy Indonesian stocks on expectations that Indonesia will get investment grade in foreseeable future," dealers say. Among gainers, \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) +8.7%; Telkom (TLKM.JK) +3.3%, \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) +7.7%; \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) +4.1%, and Bumi Resources (BUMI.JK) +5.6%. 3,250-3,350 now tipped for main index. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT [Dow Jones] China attracts $7.6 billion of actual foreign direct investment in August, +1.38% on-year, vs +29.2% at $6.92 billion in July, +39.6% in June, Ministry of Commerce says. Economists say eased growth mostly due to high comparison base in August last year; also, foreign investment may has been affected following foreign companies' repeated complaints recently of inconsistent enforcement of rules, government procurement process biased toward local companies, insufficient protection for intellectual property rights. However add, monthly fluctuations not sufficient to fully portray trend. FDI in January-August period +18.06% on-year to $65.96 billion, vs +20.65% during January-July period; FDI totaled $58.35 billion in January-July. (li.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY strength may persist in weeks ahead despite MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly if U.S. Federal Reserve moves toward further easing, Treasury yields fall ahead, says Hideki Amikura, deputy general manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. "I'd give the yen about a 60% chance of staying strong." Says while USD/JPY could rise to around 85.00, substantial selling by Japan exporters there would likely weigh on pair. Adds, any USD/JPY buying by non-Japanese short-term investors in reaction to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to continue. But says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has at least, likely given USD/JPY a support at 83.00. "That could become the support for now, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was conducted after the breach of that level."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 2339ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0002l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
