\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Yuan jumps on fixing; baht rally capped by c.bank\b0\par\par\uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 08:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Won gains with stocks as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears grow\par \par \uc2 * Dollar/yuan NDFs dip, implying slower yuan rise\par \par \uc2 * Baht inches up but capped again by c.bank\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan jumped on Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised the market by fixing the mid-point at its highest since the landmark revaluation in 2005, while the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to rein in the baht.\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks rose to a four-month high as some investors were inspired by positive U.S. and Japanese economic data to pick out bargains, with the shift to riskier assets weighing on the yen. [ID:nTOE68902E]\par \par \uc2 Trading was thinned by public holidays in Singapore, Indonesia, Malysia and the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 YUAN\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> gained nearly a quarter of a percent to 6.7669 per dollar after the PBOC set the mid-point at 6.7625 -- the highest since the landmark revaluation in 2005, despite the dollar's broad strength.\par \par \uc2 "The move took the market by shock. I think it's more political in nature," said an NDF trader in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner will present the Obama administration's latest view of what the United States should do to press China to reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices at a hearing next week. [ID:nN09225757]\par \par \uc2 But analysts reckon the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be trying to introduce two-way fluctuations in the yuan to deter market speculation.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has gained 0.8 percent since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  scrapped a dollar peg on June 19.\par \par \uc2 Offshore dollar/yuan non-delierable forwards <CNYNDFOR=> tumbled, with the benchmark one-year NDFs <CNY1YNDFOR=> falling to 6.6850 from 6.6950 at Thursday's close.\par \par \uc2 The implied 12-month yuan appreciation fell to 1.16 percent from 1.29 percent due to the sharply higher yuan mid-point.\par \par \uc2 BAHT\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> inched up to 30.80 per dollar but traders suspected dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper its appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The baht was off a 13-year peak at 30.77 hit on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The baht's strength is still prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come in, but its job is being made relatively easy this morning by holidays in Singapore, Malaysia and some other Asian countries," said a dealer in Bangkok.\par \par \uc2 "The baht should range in 30.80-90 today, but it has room to appreciate further as long as the dollar outlook remains weak," added the dealer.\par \par \uc2 The baht has gained 8 percent against the dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 WON\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> gained a fifth of a percent to 1,164.7 per dollar, the highest since Aug 10, as local stocks rose after stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data boosted investors' tolerance for risk.\par \par \uc2 Traders were growing more cautious as the South Korean authorities, worried about the stronger won's impact on export companies, could step in and buy dollars.\par \par \uc2 Seoul's main stock market index <.KS11> rose more than 1 percent for the first 30 minutes of trade as foreign investors were net buyers on the main board. CURRENICIES VS U.S. DOLLAR \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change