\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2 : Singapore, Korea In; Japan On Standby\b0\par\par\uc2 By Costas Paris and Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 988 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 10:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Asian authorities stepped up the fight to curb their strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday. Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in months and Korea resumed its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a two-week break, while Japan is increasingly signaling it might enter the market for the first time in six years.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore sold the Singapore dollar after it hit an all-time high, "to smooth the U.S. dollar's fall," said a person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking. The Bank of Korea sold won for dollars, traders said, although the greenback remained weaker on the day.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, people familiar with the matter said the government is more determined than ever to act against any unusually rapid yen rise--but would find it hard to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the gains are gradual.\par \par \uc2 The developments in Asia, which is bouncing back strongly this year from the global economic downturn, highlight the difficulties policymakers face as their strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  threaten to crimp the growth in exports that is fueling the rebound.\par \par \uc2 Some Asian central banks, such as those in Taiwan and Thailand, are in the market regularly, but all of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are aimed at merely smoothing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains, not reversing the dollar's seemingly inexorable glide downward.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been rising against the dollar this year, first as the global recovery encouraged investors to buy riskier assets and, more recently, as signs of a sharp U.S. slowdown have increased bets that the Federal Reserve will ease policy further. This decreases the attractiveness of dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  defended the greenback after it fell to a record low S$1.3425, the person told Dow Jones Newswires. The MAS, which formally targets a gradual rise in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since April, despite the Singapore dollar's rise of more than 4% against the greenback so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The MAS "will continue to smooth the U.S. dollar's fall for now, but this is a regional issue with widespread weakness for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the person said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's trading partners will probably keep rising for now, in which case the MAS's role will be "to make sure that the rise is orderly."\par \par \uc2 This indicates Singapore won't fight hard against a rise in the local dollar as long as it's broadly in line with its Asian peers.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, if Japan's starts selling yen, the aim would be to curb rapid moves, not to reverse the market trend, several people familiar with the matter told Dow Jones.\par \par \uc2 "If there are no 'excessive fluctuations' in exchange rates, and if the dollar falls against the yen only gradually, it would be very difficult to act," one of the people said. "It's unthinkable that (the government) will fight back against the market trend."\par \par \uc2 The ministry views a move by the dollar of several yen a day as worryingly volatile, people familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy have said recently.\par \par \uc2 The yen is up some 10% this year, but Tokyo is strongly signaling that if it returns to the market it won't repeat its last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  binge, when it sold Y35 trillion over the 15 months to March 2004.\par \par \uc2 Adding to Japan's caution are the prospects that the U.S. Fed will whittle away what little interest-rate gap remains between the U.S. and Japan, as well as the fact that it would be a Herculean task to move the global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where volume is now $4 trillion a day.\par \par \uc2 Still, recent tough talk on the yen from Prime Minister Naoto Kan and Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda is intended to show that Japan is on heightened alert against sharp market moves, the people said. Kan and Noda have both vowed "decisive" action when needed, language that in the past has denoted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The weight carried by that word is significantly heavy," one of the people said. Another said it shows the government is "resolutely prepared" for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one of Asia's serial \b \highlight22\uc2 interveners\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stepped back into the market Friday after two weeks on the sidelines, buying an estimated $300 million-$400 million as the dollar fell to around KRW1,176, Seoul traders said. But there too the aim was to slow the won's rise, not reverse it.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was at KRW1,177.6 late Friday, still well below its level of KRW1,180.5 late Thursday. The won is down for the year after falling sharply in the first half, but has rebounded more than 8% from its late-May low.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategies differ, of course. In Malaysia, where the ringgit has led this year's rise among developing-economy \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a 10% climb, there has been no recent sign of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which views the rise as reflecting solid economic fundamentals. The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by contrast, has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to try to smooth out the baht's ascent, at 7% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The outlier is China.\par \par \uc2 While other Asian countries are enduring strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or warily attempting to temper the rise, Beijing is hardly letting the yuan budge. China ended the yuan's two year peg to the dollar in mid-June but has hardly let the local unit appreciate since then.\par \par \uc2 In fact, thanks to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the tightly controlled yuan has given up half of the modest gains it made in the first seven weeks of the new freer policy.\par \par \uc2 -By Costas Paris in Singapore and Takashi Nakamichi in Tokyo, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4151; costas.paris@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Min-Jeong Lee in Seoul and Benjamin Low in Kuala Lumpur contributed to this report.) [ 03-09-10 0854GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100903e693000k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
