\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Up On Suspected  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ; 31.30 Cap\b0\par\par\uc2 1,503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 11:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB nudges higher to 31.16 vs 31.15 late yesterday in Asia on suspected heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after pair slipped to 31.13 early, dealers say, though trade otherwise thinning out as market becomes cautious after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says it may use certain measures, if necessary, to stem THB's rapid rise. Market also eyeing U.S. non-farm payrolls due later today. "The BOT was buying in quite large amounts, but I don't think the rebound will last and I think we could see the THB even try and test 31.00 next week," says Bangkok-based trader. Support next week tipped at 31.00, resistance at 31.30. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD is looking to put pressure on Wednesday's high at 1.2856, and a break higher towards the 1.2900 level is the main threat. The August 18 lower high at 1.2923 lies above there, and upside scope exists to the 1.618 extension target at 1.2936. Failure to break above 1.2856 would prompt downside consolidation towards Thursday's low at 1.2776, but only a sustained break below there would concern bulls. EUR/USD is at 1.2844. - By Francis Bray, Dow Jones Chief Technical Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end lower; benchmark KLCI down 0.4% at 1435.67 as blue-chips succumb to profit taking. Local dealer says current consolidation phase healthy; "there's still some gas left to power the rally." KLCI has been on winning streak over past 3 weeks, only retracing 2 days out of 15. "The KLCI may still try to punch into higher territory despite its bearish divergence indicators," says Maybank IB analyst Lee Cheng Hooi. Tips resistance at 1446, then 1458; support at 1428. Market breadth ends positive with 394 advancers vs 325 decliners; Gamuda (5398.KU) +0.8% at MYR3.61, Kencana (5122.KU) +3.2% at MYR1.62, Axiata (6888.KU) down 0.6% at MYR4.51, Maybank (1155.KU) down 0.6% at MYR8.45. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF is nudging toward the 1.3040 level which has capped the cross so far in September. Any break above here could see some weak shorts taken out, however, from a technical view BarCap says the big level to watch is 1.3150 which is where \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says it would leave stops on short positions. EUR/CHF now at 1.3027. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira is stronger in line with gains in some of the major European stock markets. USD/TRY trades at 1.5090 from 1.5135 Thursday. Meanwhile bonds are almost flat after a slightly higher than expected 0.4% rise in August inflation. The benchmark April 25, 2012 bond trades at 8.08% from 8.11% Thursday. Analysts forecast TRY will trade between 1.5075 and 1.5345 and the benchmark bond between 8.00% and 8.20%, in the short term. (yeliz.candemir@iha.com.tr)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] Most Thai government bonds lower in very thin trade. Weakness in U.S. Treasurys plus recent sharp gains in local bond market inspires mild profit-taking in medium term tenors, says local dealer. Adds price downside likely limited as fund inflows expected to continue. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.62%/2.57% vs 2.59%/2.58% late Wednesday, November 2016 quoted at 2.75%/2.70% vs 2.72%/2.68%, June 2019 quoted at 2.75%/2.70% vs 2.75%/2.67%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0911 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end +1.4% at fresh record high of 3164.277 in moderate volume, led by buying in bank, consumer-related stocks after Bank Indonesia leaves key interest rate unchanged at 6.50%, traders say. "The return of foreign funds, buying interest rate-sensitive stocks also improved overall sentiment here," says trader at First Asia Capital; expects shares to continue to gain Monday; tips resistance at 3200. Foreigners net buyers of IDR182 billion worth of shares. Among gainers: \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) +2.5% at IDR6,050, Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) +3.7% at IDR3,500 on bargain buying, while \uc2 Indosat\uc2  (ISAT.JK) +7.1% at IDR4,900 after \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  ups rating on stock to Hold from Sell. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0908 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds higher late Friday on expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold policy rates rest of year. "Bonds are a bit firmer today after the expected decision on interest rates by Bank Negara yesterday," says dealer with local bank. Bank Negara Thursday kept overnight policy rate unchanged at 2.75%. Adds, "people are still better buyers of bonds because \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hinted there won't be any more interest rate hikes this year." Also says bond markets unlikely to fluctuate too much for some time, given no impetus for profit taking or any more room for yields to come down. "Markets will probably move in a range till the second half of November," when Bank Negara meets again, dealer adds. Yields on May 2013 down 1 bp at 3.16%, August 2015 down 5 bps at 3.29%, September 2017 down 4 bps at 3.52%, November 2019 down 2 bps at 3.68%. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT [Dow Jones] The UK August Services PMI echoes the manufacturing and construction reports seen this week and signals a sharp slowdown in growth across the economy notes Capital Economics. Their own composite index suggests the overall economic recovery in the UK has pretty much ground to a halt. CE says whilst these PMI surveys tend to lead hard economic data by a few months, suggesting 3Q GDP readings may still indicate expansion, the threat of renewed contraction in 4Q and beyond, is becoming very real. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0857 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS steady in quiet trade; 1-year flat at 2.00%-2.05%, 5-year unchanged at 2.69%-2.76%. Traders says few deals done today as they see no clear direction for market. "The market isn't going to respond to possible higher August CPI as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't unlikely to hike interest rates this year," says Shanghai-based local bank trader. \uc2 UBS\uc2  expects CPI inflation to rise to 3.7% in August, new high this year. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] Who's afraid of a double dip? Surely not the executives at \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE); South Korean automaker thrived in last U.S. downturn, pitching new models to American consumers looking for inexpensive, fuel-efficient cars. Since end-2008, company's U.S. market share has climbed to 4.7% through August from 3%. Good news for Hyundai lately is gains have continued even as other automakers have regained their footing; rising reputation for quality, value means company can cut back on discounts, cheap financing. Bottom line is by end-2010, profits will have more than tripled in just two years, Daiwa Capital Markets forecasts. Sure, rapid earnings growth can't go on indefinitely; KRW expected to strengthen further this year which could crimp margins, competition from Japan's automakers too. For now though, Hyundai still gaining traction. See: "=HEARD ON THE STREET: \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2 's All-Wheel Drive." (james.simms@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0850 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR at fresh 13-year low of 3.1200 late vs 3.1265 Thursday; local dealer says better-than-expected Malaysia July export numbers, generally lower USD/Asians pushes pair down. "It's been mainly one-way traffic with a lot of (dollar) sellers," says dealer; adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen buying dollars at 3.1230, then 3.1220. "But we think it's mainly to smoothen the flow." Another dealer also suspects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Players awaiting non-farm payroll data for direction; "the general trend is still for the pair to head lower, but we can't predict the immediate knee-jerk reaction from the data," dealer adds. Tips 3.1150-3.1350 range Monday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com) [ 09-03-10 0525ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100903e6930006h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
