\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(9/3) Stronger Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Stir Worry\b0\par\par\uc2 794 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2010\par 20:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Piyarat Setthasiriphaiboon \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand and Singapore sent signals of concern Thursday about the rising value of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in the latest indication of how fluctuating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets are pressuring Asian nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Officials in Thailand held an emergency meeting in Bangkok to discuss the baht, which last week hit a two-year high against the U.S. dollar. The Thai government is concerned about the strength of the baht and has asked the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to closely monitor its movement, Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij said Thursday after the meeting.\par \par \uc2 In Singapore, a person familiar with the matter said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is poised to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the Singapore dollar approaches its record high again. The U.S. dollar traded near its lowest point of the year against the Singapore dollar Thursday before regaining some ground. For the year, the U.S. dollar is down about 4.2% against the Singapore dollar.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar has also fallen against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Year-to-date it is down 8.7% against Malaysia's ringgit, 4.4% against Indonesia's rupiah and 3.4% against the Philippine peso as of Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is down 6.6% against the Thai baht this year.\par \par \uc2 The gains in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of developing Asian nations have come as investors park money in economies with strong growth prospects amid worries about the global economy. Many investors are also betting the countries will face continued pressure to raise interest rates to keep inflation under control, a move that often drives \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.\par \par \uc2 Also, projections that the Federal Reserve will have to loosen policy further to stimulate growth is weighing on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asian authorities worry that a quick rise in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be destabilizing. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can also make a country's exports less competitive.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar's fall hasn't been as extreme against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it has against Japan's. The greenback is down nearly 10% this year against the yen and is trading around 15-year lows. Still, volatility over the past year has led countries such as the Philippines, \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Singapore to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Prime Minister Abhisit Vejjajiva called the urgent meeting Thursday with economic ministers and \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  Gov. Tarisa Watanagase to discuss the baht's rise.\par \par \uc2 After the meeting, Mr. Korn, the finance minister, said there has been no speculation detected in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but said the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  already has the necessary tools to cope with such a situation should it arise.\par \par \uc2 "The government is concerned about the strong baht," the finance minister said. "[But] overall, we haven't seen that the current strong baht has caused a problem. . . . Still-growing exports reflects that exporters are still competitive."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Tarisa also played down the impact after the meeting. "The current baht situation is not worrisome. . . . Inflows so far are the short-term flows into the debt and stock markets, but it is still unclear whether it is speculation," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said.\par \par \uc2 Commerce Minister Porntiva Nakasai recently urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do more to stabilize the baht, blaming its rise against the dollar for a sharp slowdown in export growth in July. On Thursday, Ms. Porntiva told reporters that the robust baht has hurt some exporters, especially in the food and agricultural sectors, but the ministry is working on measures to reduce the impact. The ministry, however, is maintaining its target for export growth of 20% this year, she said.\par \par \uc2 In Singapore, the person familiar with the matter said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore is prepared to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise against the U.S. dollar in the short to medium term if the move is orderly and in line with other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  watch," the person said.\par \par \uc2 This suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be imminent for the MAS, which is thought to have stayed out of the market since April. The U.S. dollar was quoted at 1.3485 Singapore dollars in late Asian trading, up from the day's low of S$1.3454 but still close to its July 15, 2008, all-time low of S$1.3438.\par \par \uc2 In recent days the Singapore dollar has been about 2% above the midpoint of the undisclosed trade-weighted band in which the MAS guides the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the person said. "Anything above that will trigger action."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses the exchange rate rather than interest rates as its main policy tool because Singapore's trade flows dwarf the island's domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 02, 2010 14:56 ET (18:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100902e692004q0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
