\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Thai Govt Should Steer Clear Of Strong Baht\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leigh Murray  \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2010\par 11:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai government is getting jittery about the strength of the baht. It had better get used to it.\par \par \uc2 With interest rates on an upward trajectory and capital inflows showing no signs of easing, the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is heading in only one direction: up. Don't expect a reversal anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Commerce Minister Porntiva Nakasai on Thursday blamed the strong baht for a sharp slowdown in export growth in July. Southeast Asia's second-largest economy recorded a 20.6% on-year rise in exports to $15.56 billion in July, but it was well off the 46.3% on-year surge to a historical high of $18.04 billion in June.\par \par \uc2 Capital inflows to the bond market alone suggest the baht will remain well bid this year, keeping export growth in check. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data show net capital inflow into bonds was around THB20 billion ($634.5 million) in each of June and July, and was close to THB50 billion year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 Brisk exports have been a boon for the Thai economy. Gross domestic product data for the second quarter is due for release Monday, with the median forecast of 11 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires tipping an expansion of 7.9% on-year, from a 15-year high of 12% in the first quarter. Needless to say the still-strong economy, surging stocks and rising interest rates are luring capital into the country and driving up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The baht is up 4.8% so far this year; the dollar was recently fetching THB31.55.\par \par \uc2 Porntiva warned the U.S. dollar may drop as low as THB30.00 this year; this is possible but the more likely scenario based on recent moves is for a gradual track down toward THB31.00 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit sharp volatility, and a renewed push below that in 2011.\par \par \uc2 The minister's pleas for the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively to cap the baht will likely fall on deaf ears, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly insisting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's been moving in line with regional peers.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  Governor Tarisa Watanagase earlier this week argued that the baht wasn't too strong in the context of gains made against the dollar by regional counterparts. The governor also said the baht's strength hasn't impeded Thailand's competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 It's clear the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which says it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out volatility, appears averse to ramp up its efforts to reverse the trend.\par \par \uc2 The BOT would rather prefer exporters take advantage of other tools, including hedging, to cope with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  pain.\par \par \uc2 The market has already been prepared for a deceleration in export growth in the second half due to an easing need for foreign companies to increase inventories which were wound down during the global financial crisis, and a softening of demand as the pace of the economic recovery slows in some regions, particularly the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to know how to play its hand in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The government should stand aside.\par \par \uc2 (Leigh Murray is Bureau Chief, Thailand & Vietnam, for Dow Jones Newswires. He has 19 years of experience as a journalist and editor, having worked for news organizations in Thailand, Singapore, Indonesia and Australia. He can be contacted on 66 2690 4200 or by email: leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 20-08-10 0911GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1171 1184 4253 5008 70779  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100820e68k000la\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
