\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Shares May Fall; Eyed In Consolidation Mode\b0\par\par\uc2 1,345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2010\par 03:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares may fall as market entering consolidation mode after recent rally. Benchmark +16.6% since end May driven by optimism over 2Q earnings, easing political tension, but SET Index running out of gas, says Globlex Securities' Head of Equity Investment Strategy Chakkrit Charoenmethachai. Adds approaching long holiday likely to prompt investors to take profit, weakness expected in banks, construction plays. Support pegged at 869, resistance at 880. Thai market to be shut Thursday, Friday for public holidays. SET Index ends flat at 875.07 Friday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped to fall as investor sentiment likely undermined by weak U.S. jobs data Friday; benchmark PSEi may to slide to immediate support at 3500 after ending 0.1% lower Friday at 3516.28. Next support at 3480, resistance at 3530. Investors likely to take profit on banks, property stocks that advanced recently on strong earnings such as Banco de Oro (BDO.PH), Ayala Land (ALI.PH). "We'll likely see profit-taking today as other markets weaken. Liquidity in the market may also be sapped as government starts this week its retail treasury bond offering," says Astro del Castillo, managing director at First Grade Holdings. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude rises 0.4% in Asian trading, recovering $81.00/bbl level after Friday's decline. Crude fell for 3 consecutive days in floor trade last week, but market sentiment turns positive as "prices were supported above $80," says Tomokazu Amano, analyst with \uc2 Mitsubishi Corporation\uc2  Futures. Adds, next focus is U.S. FOMC meeting Tuesday; "investors expect there will be some indication of additional actions to prop up the U.S. economy." Until then, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , equity movements likely to set tone for crude oil, he says. September Nymex crude last up 34 cents at $81.04/bbl on Globex. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0116 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD lower at 7.7627 vs 7.7641 late Friday, tracking broad USD weakness vs majors Friday, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in narrow 7.7610-7.7660 band near-term. "There is no significant flow in the market and investors are just keeping an eye on the euro/dollar rate for trading cues," trader says.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate for now after settling down $1.31 Friday at $80.70/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics bearish at overbought level. Support at $80.04 (Friday's low); breach would target $79.69 (previous cap set July 27), then $78.28 (100-day moving average), $77.87 (200-day moving average) and $76.83 (July 30 low). Resistance at $82.14 (hourly chart swing high); breach would target $82.67 (Friday's high), then $82.97 (Wednesday's 3-month high) - above which would open way to psychological round-numbered levels from $84.00 up to $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 top). September crude last up 26 cents at $80.96/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD may fall vs 31.819 late Friday, tracking broad USD weakness vs majors as weak jobs data out of U.S. raise expectations Fed could take further monetary easing steps. USD/TWD likely to test 31.70 support; if pair falls below that level, it may head for 31.50. Trader at local bank says players likely to keep close eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities as Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if market too volatile. Traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying (USD) to stem TWD's gains Friday. (jr.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB slips to 31.99-32.00 vs 32.08 late Friday, lowest since May 2008, on broader USD weakness following disappointing U.S. economic data. \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , slowing THB rise by buying USD since early trade, dealers say. "They (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) don't buy aggressively...It's unlikely that they will be persistent and try to reverse the bearish dollar trend," one dealer says. Another dealer adds, BOT may wait to see whether market would sell heavily after pair breaches 32.00. Support put at 31.95. Resistance at 32.05. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may open tad lower to consolidate recent gains, after index up 13 of past 14 sessions for 7.9% gain. Still, short-term bias likely to remain up on strong HK corporate earnings, with blue chips having consensus-smashing results last week; include \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  (0293.HK), \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK); index members slated to report this week include HKEx (0388.HK), China Overseas Land (0688.HK), Tencent (0700.HK), \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  (0023.HK), MTRC (0066.HK), \uc2 Li & Fung\uc2  (0494.HK). After recent rally, DBS Vickers notes HSI valuation risen to 13.3X forward P/E, but still below historical average of 14.4X; house doesn't have hard-landing scenario for China, says beyond volatility this quarter, "we believe China should be in a good position to showcase its resiliency in the face of global uncertainty, considering its strong fiscal muscle and flexibility in monetary policies." HSI +0.6% at 21,678.80 Friday; some eye 22,000 this week on expectations of more solid blue-chip results. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to extend fall below modern-era closing low Friday following USD weakness on weak U.S. jobs data; USD/CNY tipped to test 6.7600 vs 6.7683 at Friday's close. "Given what's happening in the international market, the dollar-yuan has a lot of room to fall, but it's all up to how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will control it," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. Adds little sign of PBOC in market on Friday, which "interpreted by the market to mean that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tolerating (Friday's) fall below the key 6.7700 support." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] With markets increasingly showing concern over soggy U.S. growth, and the possibility of a fresh round of quantitative easing, the prospects of USD garnering medium- term traction seem to rest on U.S. economic data starting to show some serious improvement, and quickly, says \uc2 Macquarie Bank\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy report. Adds, local job's data Thursday could well reinforce the diverging outlooks for the Australian and U.S. economies. Macquarie economics are forecasting a 15,000 increase in jobs for July versus market consensus of +20,000. Says AUD/USD topside momentum now brings into play the previous major highs between 0.9330 to 0.9410. Now 0.9179. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] IMM commitment of traders report shows net JPY long non-commercial positions vs USD rose to 47,998 contracts in week to August 3 vs 29,921 contracts week earlier, indicating investors bought JPY on growing expectations Fed may take further monetary easing steps. Still, JPY-long positions in past tend not to rise sharply above 50,000 contracts, which could mean USD/JPY's downside likely to be limited, pair may even rebound in near term. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped to open higher, likely on concerns over weak U.S. payrolls data; but traders say rise may be capped at 45.00 after settling late Friday at 44.89 as EUR, other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  still strong vs USD; support at 44.70. "We don't expect any major moves either way, although a gap-up is possible given the risk to the U.S. recovery," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 08-08-10 2129ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100809e68900012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
