\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Down To Nearly 27-Month Low;31.95 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2010\par 03:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB slips to 31.99-32.00 vs 32.08 late Friday, lowest since May 2008, on broader USD weakness following disappointing U.S. economic data. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected to have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , slowing THB rise by buying USD since early trade, dealers say. "They (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) don't buy aggressively...It's unlikely that they will be persistent and try to reverse the bearish dollar trend," one dealer says. Another dealer adds, BOT may wait to see whether market would sell heavily after pair breaches 32.00. Support put at 31.95. Resistance at 32.05. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may open tad lower to consolidate recent gains, after index up 13 of past 14 sessions for 7.9% gain. Still, short-term bias likely to remain up on strong HK corporate earnings, with blue chips having consensus-smashing results last week; include \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  (0293.HK), \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK); index members slated to report this week include HKEx (0388.HK), China Overseas Land (0688.HK), Tencent (0700.HK), \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  (0023.HK), MTRC (0066.HK), \uc2 Li & Fung\uc2  (0494.HK). After recent rally, DBS Vickers notes HSI valuation risen to 13.3X forward P/E, but still below historical average of 14.4X; house doesn't have hard-landing scenario for China, says beyond volatility this quarter, "we believe China should be in a good position to showcase its resiliency in the face of global uncertainty, considering its strong fiscal muscle and flexibility in monetary policies." HSI +0.6% at 21,678.80 Friday; some eye 22,000 this week on expectations of more solid blue-chip results. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to extend fall below modern-era closing low Friday following USD weakness on weak U.S. jobs data; USD/CNY tipped to test 6.7600 vs 6.7683 at Friday's close. "Given what's happening in the international market, the dollar-yuan has a lot of room to fall, but it's all up to how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will control it," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank. Adds little sign of PBOC in market on Friday, which "interpreted by the market to mean that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tolerating (Friday's) fall below the key 6.7700 support." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] With markets increasingly showing concern over soggy U.S. growth, and the possibility of a fresh round of quantitative easing, the prospects of USD garnering medium- term traction seem to rest on U.S. economic data starting to show some serious improvement, and quickly, says \uc2 Macquarie Bank\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy report. Adds, local job's data Thursday could well reinforce the diverging outlooks for the Australian and U.S. economies. Macquarie economics are forecasting a 15,000 increase in jobs for July versus market consensus of +20,000. Says AUD/USD topside momentum now brings into play the previous major highs between 0.9330 to 0.9410. Now 0.9179. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] IMM commitment of traders report shows net JPY long non-commercial positions vs USD rose to 47,998 contracts in week to August 3 vs 29,921 contracts week earlier, indicating investors bought JPY on growing expectations Fed may take further monetary easing steps. Still, JPY-long positions in past tend not to rise sharply above 50,000 contracts, which could mean USD/JPY's downside likely to be limited, pair may even rebound in near term. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped to open higher, likely on concerns over weak U.S. payrolls data; but traders say rise may be capped at 45.00 after settling late Friday at 44.89 as EUR, other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  still strong vs USD; support at 44.70. "We don't expect any major moves either way, although a gap-up is possible given the risk to the U.S. recovery," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi hovering around last close, last +0.1% at 1785.05, as players digest weaker than expected U.S. jobs report on Friday, also ahead of Fed, BOK rate decisions this week. \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  analyst Bae Sung-young says degree of fall limited as jobs report sparked hopes Fed will take further action to stimulate economy; "key corporate results announcements are mostly over now, so market focus is shifting to macroeconomic news...issues this week are the FOMC meeting (global day Tuesday) and Thursday's BOK policy rate decision, so the index won't move sharply until the middle of this week." Tips 1778-1788 range for day. No clear sector-wide moves, though recent underperformers gaining on valuation grounds; Hynix (000660.SE) +0.2% at KRW22,900, while \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +0.4% at KRW143,500. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.1% at 4562.3, recovering from opening fall to 4540.1 on the back of offshore weakness caused by weak U.S. jobs. Energy stocks accounting for most of the weakness, Woodside (WPL.AU) down 0.5%, Santos (STO.AU) down 1.0% on lower oil prices and Eastern Star Gas (ESG.AU) A$100 million share placement for LNG site project purchase. \uc2 JB Hi-Fi\uc2  (JBH.AU) down 1.0% on slightly disappointing results. Materials supporting the market, with \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 0.5%, Fortescue (FMG.AU) up 1.1% after offshore peers proved resilient. Banks mostly flat, while NAB (NAB.AU), AXA APH (AXA.AU), IOOF (IFL.AU) in trading halt, pending announcements. Traders mostly expecting consolidation within last week's range. "I don't see anything that's going to push as far in either direction today," says Macquarie Private Wealth client adviser James Rosenberg. "We're really just marking time before the FOMC meeting this week and Australian earnings season. There's an increasing expectation that there will be a little bit of quantitative action from the Fed, but I don't think it will be enough to move equity markets significantly." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may consolidate after sharp run-up last week, but upward momentum remains intact, dealers say; Shanghai Composite Index tipped in 2600-2700 range after ending +1.4% at 2658.39 Friday. "As the index rebounded even after falling below the psychologically important level of 2600 (last week), investors bear a certain amount of hope that more capital inflows can continue to boost the market," says Wei Daoke at Shenyin and Wanguo Securities. Shenzhen index +1.9% at 1107.44. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to fall moderately as weakness in U.S. stocks Friday drags on Asian stocks; Taiex tipped to consolidate in 7900-8000 range vs 7963.30 last close. "There's little news to push the market significantly lower today," says trader at local brokerage; "we expect the market will be in a tight range with lower trading volume." Mobile phone makers, financial holding companies tipped to outperform on expectations for strong July revenue. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian employment data Thursday likely to affirm strong picture of domestic economy (consensus +20,000 new jobs in July), but downside risk exists for AUD/USD over the week with Fed likely to disappoint calls for more quantitative easing, says Joe Capurso, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CBA. Adds, expect a firmer USD over the week. CBA's long term forecast for AUD/USD is for a fall to 0.8200 reflecting an improvement in the US economy and higher US interest rates. Pair now 0.9182. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise on improved risk appetite amid upbeat economic outlook, MYR strength; dealers say market likely to resume uptrend after bout of profit-taking last week. KLCI ended 0.1% lower at 1360.45 Friday. "Underlying sentiment is still bullish, which means the KLCI should regain its upward momentum and retest last week's high," dealer says; pegs support at 1358. KLCI reached 2.5-year high of 1370.52 last week. Carmakers such as Tan Chong (4405.KU) may rise on expectations stronger MYR would reduce cost of imported components, boosting profit margins. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 08-08-10 2110ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100809e6890000v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
