\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: Thai Fin Min: Rates Could Hit 2% In 2010, Depending On Growth\b0\par\par\uc2 539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2010\par 10:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 DAEJEON, South Korea (Dow Jones)--Thai Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij said Monday that the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's policy interest rate could go up to 2% this year, depending on the country's economic growth.\par \par \uc2 "If recovery continues at this pace, it's possible, " Korn told Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of the "Asia 21" conference here.\par \par \uc2 The government recently upped Thailand's 2010 growth forecast to 5.5%, while others--including the International Monetary Fund--aim even higher.\par \par \uc2 Korn said an upside surprise is more likely than the opposite, as investment, exports and other sectors are likely to come in stronger than forecast. Exports in particular should grow 14%-15% this year, he said.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Korn told Reuters he would be surprised if the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  doesn't raise its policy rate this week. The rate stands at 1.25% ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Committee meeting Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Thai inflation, which rose 0.26% on-month in June and 3.3% on-year, is "minor," Korn said: "There's no asset bubble in Thailand."\par \par \uc2 But the fact that Thailand now lags most of its neighbors in raising interest rates and taking other tightening steps may up the pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.\par \par \uc2 "On its own it shouldn't be the reason for (a rate hike), but the fact that a number of Asian economies have increased rates probably means" the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  will feel pressure to follow suit, he said. The notion of being tardy "might give some discomfort to those who want rates to be raised."\par \par \uc2 Even a hike wouldn't hurt Thailand's growth prospects, Korn said. But he did identify several other risks.\par \par \uc2 "One is how the economies of trading partners perform," given the slow rebound in the U.S. and Europe. "And the second issue is related to domestic politics."\par \par \uc2 Anti-government protests this spring paralyzed Bangkok for several months until authorities launched a bloody crackdown in May. Some fear the long standoff could damage Thailand's appeal to tourists and investors.\par \par \uc2 "It's a risk," Korn said. "We won't deny" that the political unrest could have "significantly impacted" the Thai economy.\par \par \uc2 "Unless we find a way of resolving differences," the country is at risk of "repeating this conflict in the future," he said. "The global community will have no reason to have confidence in us."\par \par \uc2 Elections "almost certainly" won't be held this year, but "sometime next year," Korn said.\par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Korn said the baht "fairly reflects the fundamentals of the economy." The dollar was at 32.36 baht as of 0815 GMT Monday, a gain of 3.24% for the Thai unit against the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 While the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been known to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Korn noted that the country's exports are thriving because "our products are now competitive"--and not, by implication, because the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is artificially held below its true value.\par \par \uc2 -By P.R. Venkat and Kanga Kong; venkat.pr@dowjones.com +65 64154 152;\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 12, 2010 04:42 ET (08:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100712e67c001cl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
