\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Asia Reserves Creep Up, Countries Look To Diversify\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2010\par 07:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose 1% in June from the previous month, bouncing back after a steep drop in May, as Asian central banks bought more U.S. dollars to cap gains in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rise, an indication that the fast-growing region keeps attracting capital inflows despite concerns of a global economic slowdown, highlights the dilemma faced by Asian authorities as they look for ways to reduce their exposure to U.S. Treasurys--as China appears to be doing on a small scale, by stepping up its purchases of Japanese bonds.\par \par \uc2 Reserves held by 11 key Asian central banks, excluding China's, amounted to US$2.803 trillion at the end of June, up from US$2.776 trillion at the end of May, but remained just below an all-time hit in April, according to calculations by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Reserve accumulation in June came as the euro remained essentially steady against the dollar--following a 7.5% drop in May--and minor reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen and the British pound posted significant gains, boosting the dollar value of securities denominated in those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen and the pound together are estimated to account for around 8% of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in developing countries, on average, compared with almost 30% for the euro. However, speculation over increased \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest in these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased this week after news that China poured twice as much money into Japanese government bonds in the first four months of 2010 than its record full-year purchases in 2005.\par \par \uc2 This move underscores a willingness of some Asian countries to diversify their reserves into \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the greenback and the battered euro, at a time when countries from \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to South Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down and protect the competitive edge of their exporters. In recent years, this diversification has been done predominantly by purchasing euro-denominated securities.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the dollar's role as paramount reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains safe, as the yen and the pound present drawbacks that should stop them from becoming major challengers, including uncertain valuation prospects since Japan is looking for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.K. faces a dire fiscal picture.\par \par \uc2 Still, a small, slow increase in yen allocations going forward is possible, said Tim Condon, chief Asian economist at ING Bank, noting that yields on U.S. Treasurys and top euro bonds have fallen towards the low levels of Japanese government bonds, removing a key obstacle that has made yen holdings unattractive for years.\par \par \uc2 "If G-3 rates remain in this sort of low level convergence, then Japanese bonds will be relatively more attractive, and holdings of Japanese bonds should rise," Condon added.\par \par \uc2 International Monetary Fund data show the yen has never accounted for more than 9% of global official reserves -- well below the 87% peak hit by the dollar in 1976, and its 61.5% share in the first quarter this year -- while the pound has been below the 5% mark since 1974.\par \par \uc2 China, which releases its reserves data on a quarterly basis, reported US$2.447 trillion in reserves at the end of March and is expected to announce second-quarter figures in the next few days.\par \par \uc2 Following are the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for Asia's 12 leading economies. All numbers are in billions of U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 The total number excludes China.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par             Jun 10       Jun 09       May 10     As Of \par TOTAL        2,803       2,535         2,776   end June Ex China \par  \par China        2,447       2,130         2,447   end March \par Japan        1,050       1,019         1,041   end June \par India          277       264.6           272   25-Jun \par Indonesia     76.3        57.6          75.6   end June \par Korea        274.2       231.7         270.2   end June \par Malaysia      94.8        91.5          95.5   end June \par Pakistan      15.8        11.8            16   25-Jun \par Philippines   48.4        39.6          47.7   end June \par Singapore      200       173.2         198.4   end June \par Taiwan       362.4       317.6         360.1   end June \par Thailand     147.1       121.6         143.4   25-Jun \par Hong Kong    256.8         207         256.2   end June \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (David Roman, a regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve