\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won, ringgit lead Asia gains; rupiah up on \uc2 Moody's\b0\par\par\uc2 1,153 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2010\par 09:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Won up 2.6 pct vs dlr, biggest pct gain in 14 months\par \par \uc2 * Ringgit hits near 2-mth high\par \par \uc2 * Authorities seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains\par \par \uc2 By Cheon Jong-woo\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, June 21 (Reuters) - China's vow to allow more yuan flexibility boosted Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday with the won appreciating the most in 14 months as the plan is seen bolstering demand in Asia's second-biggest economy and lifted risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's rupiah found further support after Moody's Investors Service raised the country's rating outlook.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in Asia were spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check gains, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to rise further, dealers and analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "Until we get risk off and the yuan flips above 6.83 again, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to rise," a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer in Kuala Lumpur said.\par \par \uc2 Spot yuan strengthened to 6.8015 to the dollar, compared with Friday's close of 6.8262, even after the People's Bank of China set Monday's mid-point <CNY=SAEC> unchanged at 6.8275 before trading started.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, China said it would gradually make the yuan's exchange rate more flexible, indicating it was ready to break a 23-month-old U.S. dollar peg that had come under intense fire.\par \par \uc2 The move eased tension with the West ahead of a summit of the Group of 20 countries this week, encouraging investors to snap up riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 (For more on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , see [ID:nSGE65I02M])\par \par \uc2 WON\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> posted its biggest daily percentage gain against the dollar in nearly 14 months as China's vow to allow more yuan flexibility boosted risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 The won found further support from exporters' demand for settlements, which prompted investors to clear dollar-long positions to cut losses, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were spotted to curb its strength.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended local trade up 2.61 percent at 1,172.0 per dollar from Friday's domestic close <KRW=KFTC> of 1,202.6.\par \par \uc2 The percentage gain was the biggest since a 4.58 percent rise on April 30, 2009.\par \par \uc2 "Support from the yuan's news was stronger than expected. The authorities tried to curb the won's strength around the end of local trade, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not strong enough," said a foreign bank dealer.\par \par \uc2 Seoul shares <.KS11> closed up 1.62 percent with foreign investors buying a net 293.0 billion won worth of stocks on the main exchange.\par \par \uc2 RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit added gains to hit a near two-month high, tracking its Asian peers after China's pledge over the weekend to allow more yuan flexibility.\par \par \uc2 The ringgit rose as much as 2.31 percent to 3.1780 per dollar, strongest since April 27.\par \par \uc2 "China's move boosts all Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I think USD/MYR may head to this year's low of 3.17, maybe over a course of a week," says a dealer in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2 RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> rose as \uc2 Moody's\uc2  lifted the country's rating outlook to positive from stable, praising its stability and the effectiveness of its fiscal and monetary policies. [ID:nTOE65K03M]\par \par \uc2 The rupiah was up 0.91 percent to 9,005 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to rise further on the brighter outlook for the economy, but market players remain cautious over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength.\par \par \uc2 "The Indonesian economy fundamentally is in good shape," said a foreign bank dealer in Jakarta. "I want to check 9,000, the last strong support, when Bank Indonesia keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect rapid volatility movements. If it breaks through the level, definitely rupiah will go to 8,900."\par \par \uc2 BAHT\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> tracked stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gaining more than a third of a percent to a near-six-week high of 32.28-30 per dollar after China's move.\par \par \uc2 "The baht's tone is strong but the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  is actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold it at 32.30. If the baht cannot be stabilised at this level, it might firm to as far as 32.00 later," a trader in Bangkok said.\par \par \uc2 A second trader said dollar selling pressure in early trade came mostly from foreign investors while Thai exporters held back to assess the trend. Dollar/baht was bid at 32.26 against 32.38 late on Friday. The baht has gained 3.29 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 YUAN\par \par \uc2 Market players are keen to sell dollar/yuan NDFs even with the slight bounce from the lows after the PBOC kept the yuan's mid-point flat, prompting some short-covering in early trade, dealers in Hong Kong said.\par \par \uc2 Large selling interest was seen on any move up in NDFs <CNYNDFOR=>, with U.S. banks and hedge funds doing the bulk of the selling for now. Trading was concentrated on the one-month and the one-year tenors, but overall volume was not too large.\par \par \uc2 "Though the pace of the selling has declined sharply from last week, people are still expecting an eventual move on the yuan in the next 1-2 weeks," said a senior NDF trader at a broker in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 One-year NDFs traded at 6.6280. A head trader at a European bank in HK said there was good two-way flow around 6.63 in one-year NDFs and that players were looking for opportunities to sell both USD/CNY NDFs and USD/Asia pairs. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0605 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        90.63          90.67       +0.04\par  Sing dlr        1.3738         1.3860       +0.89\par  Taiwan dlr      31.765         32.190       +1.34\par  Korean won     1172.00        1202.60       +2.61\par  Baht             32.26          32.38       +0.37\par  Peso             45.50          45.89       +0.86\par  Rupiah         9005.00        9087.00       +0.91\par  Rupee            45.62          46.16       +1.18\par  Ringgit         3.1840         3.2515       +2.12\par  Yuan            6.8028         6.8291       +0.39\par  Change so far in 2009\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        90.63          92.90       +2.50\par  Sing dlr        1.3738         1.4053       +2.29\par  Taiwan dlr      31.765         32.030       +0.83\par  Korean won     1172.00        1164.50       -0.64\par  Baht             32.26          33.32       +3.29\par  Peso             45.50          46.20       +1.54\par  Rupiah         9005.00        9420.00       +4.61\par  Rupee            45.62          46.54       +2.02\par  Ringgit         3.1840         3.4220       +7.47\par