\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won, ringgit jump on EU plan, baht capped by c.bank\b0\par\par\uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2010\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Massive EU, IMF emergency package revives confidence\par \par \uc2 * Won, ringgit lead Asian rally as risk appetite recovers\par \par \uc2 * Baht's strength sparks official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Dollar/yuan NDFs jump but investors cautious\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, May 10 (Reuters) - The South Korean won and Malaysian ringgit jumped over 2 percent each on Monday after the European Union and IMF unveiled a massive $1 trillion package to help stave off a debt crisis in the euro zone.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to slow the baht's rise.\par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=> rallied and Asian stocks rose as investors bought back riskier assets, hopinh the emergency rescue package would stabilise world financial markets and prevent Greece's debt crisis from spreading. [ID:nSGE64802Q]\par \par \uc2 WON\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> closed up nearly 2.1 percent at 1,132.1 per dollar, the biggest percentage gain since April 30, 2009.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors were not spotted buying dollars despite selling local stocks, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody is looking for dollar-short positions and the won is more likely to rise further, given the market sentiment," said a local bank dealer.\par \par \uc2 Seoul shares <.KS11> were up 1.8 percent in late trade.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, dollar/won NDFs <KRWNDFOR=> tumbled across tenors as investors unloaded riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 One-month NDFs fell to 1,129.1 from 1,155.9 late on Friday, when the most liquid NDFs hit a three-month high at 1,170.\par \par \uc2 "The market is awaiting details of the EU bailout plan, but generally risk is back on," says a Singapore-based NDF trader.\par \par \uc2 RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=> jumped to 3.2015 per dollar, rebounding sharply from six-week low on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The ringgit is boosted by improved sentiment, which makes dollar/Asians heavy this morning," said a trader in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2 On the daily chart, the slow stochastics points to near-term gains in ringgit, which has rebounded from being oversold against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 One-month dollar/ringgit NDFs <MYR1MNDFOR=> fell to 3.1910 from 3.2725 late on Friday.\par \par \uc2 BAHT\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> gained almost 0.7 percent to 32.11 per dollar, prompting \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb and smooth its gains, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 "Its agents were spotted in the market buying dollars at around 32.23, its highest level today. The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  tried to smooth its volatility," said a Bangkok-based trader.\par \par \uc2 The baht has appreciated 3.4 percent against the dollar so far this year despite domestic political turbulence.\par \par \uc2 Thai Prime Minister Abhisit Vejjajiva demanded that anti-government protesters respond by Monday to his offer of an early election, running out of patience after attacks that killed two police officers at the weekend. [ID:nSGE64901U].\par \par \uc2 YUAN\par \par \uc2 Dollar/yuan non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) tumbled, relinquishing some gains from a rally over the previous two sessions to 8-month highs.\par \par \uc2 But traders say the market remained concerned by euro zone debt problems, with little impetus for NDFs to drop further near term.\par \par \uc2 "China may still want to monitor such a complicated problem before deciding on any new policy moves," said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 One-year dollar/yuan NDFs <CNY1YNDFOR=> fell to 6.6680, pricing in 2.38 percent appreciation of the yuan in 12 months, compared with 6.7110 at Friday's close, or a 1.73 percent gain, and 6.7700 at Friday's high, for a rise of only 0.84 percent.\par \par \uc2 The spot yuan <CNY=CFXS> remained fixed near 6.83 per dollar, but investors expect the authorities to abandon the dollar-peg later this year to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains to help fight inflation. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day at   0657 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        92.97          91.56       -1.52\par  Sing dlr        1.3775         1.3955       +1.31\par  Taiwan dlr      31.505         31.850       +1.10\par  Korean won     1131.80        1155.40       +2.09\par  Baht             32.25          32.32       +0.22\par  Peso             45.17          45.53       +0.80\par  Rupiah         9085.00        9185.00       +1.10\par  Rupee            45.00          45.48       +1.06\par  Ringgit         3.2010         3.2680       +2.09\par  Yuan            6.8257         6.8257       +0.00\par  Change so far in 2010\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        92.97          92.90       -0.08\par  Sing dlr        1.3775         1.4053       +2.02\par  Taiwan dlr      31.505         32.030       +1.67\par  Korean won     1131.80        1164.50       +2.89\par  Baht             32.25          33.32       +3.32\par  Peso             45.17          46.20       +2.28\par  Rupiah         9085.00        9420.00       +3.69\par  Rupee            45.00          46.54       +3.41\par  Ringgit         3.2010         3.4220       +6.90\par  Yuan            6.8257         6.8270       +0.02\par \f28 