\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ringgit, baht at multi-wk lows, yuan forwards rise\b0\par\par\uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2009\par 08:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar strength weighs on ringgit, baht and peso\par \par \uc2 * Charts suggest peso is near strong support\par \par \uc2 * Yuan rises further in non-deliverable forwards\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Dec 23 (Reuters) - The Malaysian ringgit and Thai baht fell to multi-week lows on Wednesday as the dollar held broadly firm, while the Chinese yuan inched up in offshore forwards trading to extend gains from the previous day.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar rose to a two-month peak against the yen <JPY=> and stayed firm against the euro <EUR=>.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was buoyed by data showing the biggest rise in existing home sales in November in nearly three years, fuelling market speculation that the Federal Reserve may start to tighten monetary policy before either the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the Bank of Japan. [ID:nSGE5BM03B]\par \par \uc2 RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=> eased to 3.442 per dollar to match an eight-week low on Tuesday, but found support at that level from local exporter sales of dollars to take it to 3.4375.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been testing its 100-day moving average of 3.44 this week and some traders expect it to make a decisive break through the support level to reach 3.45 next month.\par \par \uc2 "We see a 70 percent chance it will reach there in January. Dollar/ringgit is being held back by exporters currently," said a Kuala Lumpur-based trader.\par \par \uc2 The ringgit has lost almost 2 percent in the past month.\par \par \uc2 One-month dollar/ringgit NDFs <MYR1MNDFOR=> edged up to 3.4366, virtually at par with the spot.\par \par \uc2 PESO\par \par \uc2 The peso <PHP=> eased to 46.59 per dollar, but the losses were mitigated by fresh remittance inflows in the run up to the Christmas and New Year holidays.\par \par \uc2 "The onshore market is still seeing remittance inflows. At the same time, most banks onshore are likely to convert earnings on foreign assets and foreign subsidiaries back into peso at year-end," said a dealer in Manila.\par \par \uc2 The peso has lost 1 percent this month.\par \par \uc2 On the daily chart, the peso has been supported by the 55-day moving average near 46.70, and the chance of testing 47, or the 38.2 percent retracement of its three-month rally from early September, looked slim, traders said.\par \par \uc2 YUAN\par \par \uc2 Dollar/yuan NDFs <CNYNDFOR=> dropped further after Tuesday's plunge, the biggest in a day this year.\par \par \uc2 One-year NDFs fell slightly to 6.644 at 0612 GMT compared with Tuesday's close of 6.6500. The one-year dropped 0.7 percent on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 A trader said mainly U.S. names and hedge funds had sold NDFs on Tuesday. Chinese names only joined the selling late in the Asian day.\par \par \uc2 The spot yuan <CNY=CFXS> was steady at 6.8283 per dollar on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "I really don't think the drop yesterday was mainly because of rate-rise expectations. People often make use of this timing, when everyone steps back," said a Hong Kong-based NDF trader.\par \par \uc2 BAHT\par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=> hit a one-month low of 33.35 per dollar in thin early trade, prompting suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We are probably seeing the December effect as everyone tries to close year-end positions. The dollar strength is broadly supported by positive U.S. data and the baht (will) simply track Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  moving in the 33.20-40 range between now and the New Year," a trader in Bangkok said. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day at   0636 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        91.73          91.84       +0.12\par  Sing dlr        1.4108         1.4070       -0.27\par  Taiwan dlr      32.344         32.355       +0.03\par  Korean won     1182.65        1179.90       -0.23\par  Baht             33.28          33.25       -0.11\par  Peso             46.52          46.49       -0.06\par  Rupiah         9495.00        9485.00       -0.11\par  Rupee            46.84          46.80       -0.09\par  Ringgit         3.4360         3.4310       -0.15\par  Yuan            6.8283         6.8281       -0.00\par  Change so far in 2009\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        91.73          90.60       -1.23\par  Sing dlr        1.4108         1.4340       +1.64\par  Taiwan dlr      32.344         32.860       +1.60\par  Korean won     1182.65        1259.50       +6.50\par  Baht             33.28          34.80       +4.57\par  Peso             46.52          47.52       +2.15\par  Rupiah         9495.00       11000.00      +15.85\par  Rupee            46.84          48.71       +3.99\par  Ringgit         3.4360         3.4500       +0.41\par  Yuan            6.8283         6.8230       -0.08\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Clare Jim in HONG KONG and Vithoon Amorn in BANGKOK)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091226e5cn00026\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
