\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Growth of Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves Should Slow -  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA\b0\par\par\uc2 1,275 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2009\par 10:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0902 GMT [Dow Jones] Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rise 2.1% on month in November, boosted by increase in value of non-dollar holdings and continued official purchases of dollars in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets; pace of growth expected to slow ahead as signs of dollar stability emerge and governments \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  less. All countries but Malaysia reported reserves well above year-earlier levels; biggest monthly increases seen in places where central banks \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily, such as Hong Kong, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and South Korea. With \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  estimating that 10 key emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including South Korean won and Chinese yuan, remain significantly undervalued vs dollar, reserves in most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can still gain as valuations are corrected. See =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: Asia Reserves Up 2.1% In Nov; Dollar Outlook Is Key LINK: system=djm;xrf="SRW06963" (MSA)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD's break below its 9 month uptrend at 1.4897 and then the mid-November low of 1.4801 confirms a top is in place at 1.5145 says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Axel Rudolph. He now looks for slippage toward 1.4675 and 1.4625 and if the latter gives way, 1.4480. EUR/USD now at 1.4815. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0852 GMT [Dow Jones] USD-funded carry trade has legs, even as recent data lead some to talk about when Fed may raise rates; for greenback, economic fundamentals, rate differentials each play a role, and in both cases there's no screaming reason to think USD's appeal as funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will disappear. Investors have been borrowing in low-yielding USD to put into higher-yielding assets, whether as pure \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade into likes of AUD, or foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated debt or stocks. With U.S. rates at rock-bottom, country's economy lagging, such trades have been gaining appeal with Australia raising rates, other central banks in Asia starting to inch towards tightening, but Friday's surprisingly strong U.S. payrolls, coupled with oversold signals on USD, have some talking about potential for a more prolonged USD rebound in coming weeks and months, with carry trades unwound. Not so fast. See =MONEY TALKS: Don't Get Carried Away On The US Dollar. (RXM)\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  expects Greek government bonds and other "spread products" to underperform German bunds at least until mid-January, and an overweight position should therefore be kept in bunds and French OATs within the euro zone government bond market, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist David Schnautz. Adds that Monday's very successful six-month German bubill auction supports \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 's view that core government bonds with the euro zone, and German government securities in particular, will see healthy demand. (BEM)\par \par \uc2 0850 GMT [Dow Jones] The ruble is now the worst-performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a monthly basis, having fallen by 4.6%, notes \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . This is odd, given that oil has been stable of late. A year-end fiscal spending spree, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and firmer USD have all put RUB under pressure, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. Still, this could offer a nice buying opportunity and \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says it's looking to add USD/RUB shorts at the start of next year. USD/RUB trades at 30.18. (KJM)\par \par \uc2 0847 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 12 almost flat at 136 bps vs yesterday's 137 due to lack of fresh trading cues. As JPY rise, stock weakness -- main movers of Japan credit market in a past few weeks -- seem to near end, CDS spreads also gradually narrowing, though at very slow pace; some traders expect benchmark CDS index could fall to as low as 130 bps this week. Single-name CDS deals include \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 . (6758.TO) at 60 bps vs 70 bps on Nov. 26, \uc2 Nippon Steel Corp\uc2 . (5401.TO) at 55 bps vs 60 bps on Nov. 27, \uc2 Nikon Corp\uc2 . (7731.TO) at 50 bps, \uc2 Hitachi Ltd\uc2 . (6501.TO) at 95 bps vs 94 bps on Dec. 3, \uc2 Mitsui O.S.K. Lines Ltd\uc2 . (9104.TO) at 125 bps vs 90 bps on May 22. (MEF)\par \par \uc2 0843 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds steady late, yields stuck in 1-2 bps range dealer says. "The redemption of the June 2012 didn't move the market as the amount was not significant." Bank Negara said earlier today plans to redeem MYR500 million worth of callable bond at par on December 15. Tender size of re-opening of Feb 2012 MGS at MYR2.5 billion "within market expectations". Yields for February 2015 MGS down 2 bp at 3.76%, November 2019s +1 bp at 4.25%, May 2013s down 1 bp at 3.23%. (KPL)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS flat or lower, consolidating after yesterday's sharp gains. 1-year unchanged at 2.05%-2.11%, 5-year down 1 bp at 3.55%-3.64%. "I think the swaps will extend gains in the coming sessions as the macroeconomic data to be released soon will likely send out positive signals," says Beijing-based local bank trader. (WYL)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] "Credit bull" theme in Asia likely to continue in 2010, with market participants taking "long" position in region given its "long-term above-par growth," says Calyon credit analyst Brayan Lai in report. However, says best buying opportunity now over; "fundamentals are expected to supersede technicals going forward as it is no longer justifiable - given valuations - to simply buy on the government liquidity (or) technical backdrop," he says. Expects all eyes fixed on U.S. as well as exact stage of global recovery. (DLZ)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD up late at 32.350 (highest since Nov. 20) vs 32.280 late yesterday, also higher vs around 32.234 minutes before suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed USD higher in late session (pair trades as low as 32.199), two traders say. "It looks like the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is defending the 32.300 level," says trader at government-owned bank. Another trader estimates \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys US$100 million in late session, tips pair in 32.250-32.400 band tomorrow. (ABP)\par \par \uc2 0833 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts open lower Tuesday as the tide of uncertainty rises in regard to the UK's credit worthiness. Overnight the credit rating agency \uc2 Moody's\uc2  warned the AAA ratings at UK and the US was not as safe as that of Germany, France and Canada. Although acknowledging that the main political parties realized the need to reduce the deficit, actions must follow "in the not-too-distant future to continue to provide support for the rating," it says. UK BRC November retail sales rose 1.8% on the year, less than the 2.8% forecast. UK October industrial production and manufacturing data are due at 0930 GMT, and the CBI industrial trends release is due at 1100 GMT. Also, the DMO is scheduled to tap GBP3.75 billion UKT 3.75% September 2019. The March gilt futures contract is down 0.16 on the day at 116.79. The 10-year benchmark gilt is down 0.13 at 106.175, yielding 3.704%. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0832 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  says it's entering a long USD/JPY position at current levels (89.05) to target 92.30, with a stop at 88.40. Bank sees US rates heading higher well before the BOJ moves. In addition, the pullback in the last two days provides a neat entry point, bank says. Also warns that ratings concerns may start to build for Japan. (KJM) [ 12-08-09 0402ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091208e5c80005u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
