\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Asian Central Banks  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  To Limit Dollar's Slide\b0\par\par\uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2009\par 06:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and updates various levels.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Roman \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Philippines, Thailand, Singapore and South Korea appeared to sell their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, fending off the dollar's slide that threatens their export-oriented economies. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were small, reflecting a view that Asian authorities are powerless in halting a broad sell-off in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii, meanwhile, expressed concern about a rapid rise in the yen, which soared to a 14-year high against the dollar. He said "now is the time to be vigilant" and repeated he would consider action against any "abnormal" moves in the exchange rate--though he didn't characterize Thursday's move as such.\par \par \uc2 Asian central banks, with the exception of Japan, have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets frequently in recent months trying to temper gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  driven by the swooning dollar. But officials have indicated that they can do little to turn around the trend, given the causes of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness derive from the Federal Reserve's super-easy monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Traders said Asian authorities would likely continue to do what they can to curb gains in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have also benefited from the region's fast economic recovery from last year's global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "The (falling) dollar trend is the number one reason" that keeps Asian central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Sean Callow, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . He added that such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to continue as long as the dollar remains under pressure.\par \par \uc2 The dollar took a heavy pounding overnight, with its value against a trade-weighted basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropping to a 15-month low, and the euro surging nearly two cents, cracking above $1.51.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought between $10 million and $50 million around PHP46.65 to the dollar, as the greenback hovered close to a one-month low against the Philippine peso, according to local traders. At 0531 GMT, the dollar was trading at PHP46.72.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the dollar fell to a fresh 17-month low against the Thai baht. The dollar last traded at THB33.08, compared with THB33.17-THB33.18 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "Players are well aware of the bearish tone of the dollar....The dollar-baht may slip through 33.00 near term," said one dealer, adding that the (Thai) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to try and reverse the tide.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the U.S. dollar, two people familiar with the situation said. One person said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  around S$1.3797. He added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue supporting the greenback if needed.\par \par \uc2 In early trading, the U.S. dollar was quoted as low as S$1.3774 and was last trading at S$1.3799.\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, the local authorities were suspected of buying an undetermined amount of dollars at around KRW1,153, a trader said. The dollar last traded at KRW1,154.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar last traded at Y86.55, off an intra-day low of Y86.27.\par \par \uc2 Masafumi Yamamoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 , said Japanese authorities are now more likely to step into the market to curb the yen's rise, after the government last week announced that Japan is in deflation.\par \par \uc2 "A stronger yen will hurt Japanese stocks, which is bad for the government's approval rate," Yamamoto said. "And a stronger yen will also worsen deflation."\par \par \uc2 Yamamoto added that Japan probably needs to consult with its key trade partners, like the U.S., if it intends to make such a move.\par \par \uc2 "For now, what we will be watching is whether the government's script on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will change," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By David Roman, Dow Jones Newswires, 65 6415 4045, david.roman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Piyarat Setthasiriphaiboon in Bangkok, Cris Larano in Manila, Costas Paris and P.R. Venkat in Singapore, and Takashi Nakamichi and Takashi Mochizuki in Tokyo contributed to this story.) [ 26-11-09 0545GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091126e5bq000cg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
