\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Steady; Bias Neutral, Tight Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,268 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2009\par 02:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB little changed at 33.23 vs 33.24 late yesterday in lackluster trade. As year-end holidays approaching, many players, especially foreign, unlikely to build up positions unless fresh cue emerges, says local dealer; adds this may influence pair to move either way easily. Support pegged at 33.20 with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  likely to watch markets, not expected to actively \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support THB. Resistance tipped at 33.25 as selling by exporters expected to cap. (PIY)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] Any HSI rebound likely capped as concerns of capital-raising for China banks may linger in short term, says ICBC International's Ernie Hon; tips "boring market conditions" likely given lack of fresh catalysts for index to convincingly breach 23,000, while rising 50-day moving average, now at 21,757, offering strong support for any pullback. China banks may stabilize after yesterday's selloff on fund-raising worries. Near-term focus likely on new listings with Sany (0631.HK), Fantasia (1777.HK) making debut today, while Minsheng Bank (1988.HK) - biggest HK IPO this year - will start trading tomorrow. HSI ended down 1.5% at 22,423.14 yesterday.(RLI)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares rise 0.7% to 7767.18 early, lifted mainly by rebounds in flat-panel titles in part on anticipation of growing sales of flat-screen TVs ahead of Christmas holiday season, says David Li, trader at Daiwa Securities SMBC-Cathay. \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) up 1.6% to NT$32, while rival Chi Mei (3009.TW) gains 3.7% to NT$20.95. \uc2 Compal Electronics\uc2  (2423.TW) rises 2.0% to NT$43.25 after company says 4Q laptop shipments to be higher than previously estimated. Tire makers including Cheng Shin (2105.TW) up 3.1% to NT$76 as investors expect them to raise prices on exports to U.S. after U.S. imposes anti-dumping taxes on tires from China, adds Li. Tips 7800 nearest cap for index. (PEL)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD falls to 32.240 vs 32.288 late yesterday on foreign fund inflows into local shares (Taiex +0.7%), says local bank trader. Tips pair in 32.200-32.300 band rest of morning. "Taiwan shares rose despite early losses (in Korean shares), so more foreign investors are buying local shares today...thus the Taiwan dollar still has room to rise," he says. Adds exporters likely to sell at 32.250 and above. But losses limited by importers buying below 32.200. (CMK)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR tad lower at 9,490 vs 9,500 late yesterday, taking cues from USD's broad weakness, dealers say; but pair unlikely to lose much ground from here as local companies likely to bid for month-end demand. 9,460-9,510 day range tipped. (IMS)\par \par \uc2 0124 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December Kospi200 futures down 0.2% at 211.60, turning lower after hitting day's high of 213.05 due to continued local retail investors' futures selling; but foreigners net buyers, preventing further sharp fall. "Local retail investors continue to focus on speculative day trading, while foreigners maintain directionless trade although they are biased towards buying rather than selling," says Moon Ju-hyun at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 ; expects lead Kospi200 to drift, torn between expectation for year-end rally, reality market doesn't have any fresh momentum. Expects China markets to determine market mood rest today with immediate support likely at 211, near yesterday's intraday low. (SKS)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures moderately higher, helped by weaker domestic stocks; 3-year yield down 2 bps at 4.26%, 5-year yield down 2 bps at 4.76%, December futures +6 ticks at 109.76. "Bonds succeeded in staging a rebound from intraday losses yesterday, which is supporting the mood as well," says local securities firm trader. But notes, further upside capped as players remain cautious over possibility of foreign investors turning net sellers in futures market after being buyers for most of sessions in November. Foreign investor net selling 1,049 contracts so far today. "So long as foreigners don't increase their selling, bonds could see more upside, but if otherwise, investors would become increasingly cautious." Tips 3-year yield in 4.24%-4.30% range. (MJL)\par \par \uc2 0114 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped higher, likely to shadow stronger USD around Asia, further gains probably limited to below 47.20; pair may initially rise towards 47.15 after settlling at 47.06 late Tuesday, support 46.80, resistance 47.20; "The dollar is strong across Asia this early, but at 47.15" it becomes top heavy, says a local bank trader. (SFL)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY falling, now at 88.36, initial support at 88.00 with good chance of breaking below the mark and opens new way to 87.50 in global day, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Says Japan exporters selling for their regular month-end settlement; expects such selling flow to continue this week. Adds speculators also selling due to narrower gap between U.S. and Japan long-term interest rates, a sign market sees Fed won't take any exit strategies anytime soon. EUR/USD last 132.27, dealers tip support at 131.50. (TMO)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.2% at 4699.8 as BHP (BHP.AU) rises 1.3% and major banks find support after early weakness. Southern Cross Equities says there's no chance of BHP launching another bid for \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) when U.K. Takeovers Panel standstill rule expires Friday. Broker also tips large scale share buyback from BHP in February because it would re-rate BHP's share price and take heat out of any potential takeover targets. Southern Cross says Rio should trade on a minimum 30% discount to BHP because of asset quality, management quality, balance sheet quality, commodity mix and trading liquidity. "When the hedge fund world starts working out BHP isn't coming for RIO, you will see this short BHP, long RIO trade unwind and BHP start moving to its rightful premium to RIO," says broker. Rio last up 2 cents at A$71.91. (DWR)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD flat at 7.7501 despite HKMA sell HK$5.81 billion overnight in NY trade to curb HKD. "The inflows won't abate in the near term due to the safe haven status of Hong Kong dollar under \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. I expect more HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come, " says trader at UK bank. Tips pair in 7.7500-7.7503 band today. (YKS)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's Finance Minister Fujii says both financial, monetary policy steps should be taken to fight ongoing deflation, slightly toning down his requests to BOJ, aimed at watering down speculation that government may be putting pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take additional steps. Fujii Tuesday said that deflation should be addressed primarily by monetary policy. But change doesn't necessarily mean that government has given up encouraging bank to take some steps to defeat deflation. Since Tokyo declares deflation for 1st time since 2006 Friday, expectations have grown that BOJ may purchase more JGBs to help curb long-term yields as government expected to issue more debt to implement further economic-stimulus measures to boost domestic demand. (TTA) [ 11-24-09 2033ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091125e5bp0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pi