\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -China and capital control fears hurt rupiah, rupee\b0\par\par\uc2 1,103 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2009\par 04:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Rupiah falls 1 pct on renewed capital control jitters\par \par \uc2 * Peso, ringgit fall as dollar rebounds against euro\par \par \uc2 * Baht weaker after c.bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap its early rise\par \par \uc2 * Indian rupee NDFs price in less appreciation\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 19 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah and Indian rupee slipped in spot and offshore markets on Thursday on jitters of possible official steps to curb capital inflows after Brazil's latest attempt to limit the rise of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also headed lower as the dollar rebounded against the euro after a sell-off on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Brazil took another step on Wednesday to try to contain the appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unveiling a 1.5 percent tax on certain trades involving American Depositary Receipts issued by Brazilian companies. [ID:nN18128104]\par \par \uc2 Taiwan has banned foreigners from investing in time deposits and Indonesia has talked of limiting foreign ownership of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, raising concerns emerging markets might impose a wave of capital controls.\par \par \uc2 RUPEE\par \par \uc2 Dollar/rupee non-deliverable forwards moved higher in line with the spot market as fears of other Asian countries following Brazil's lead and imposing capital controls on foreign fund inflows weigh on sentiment.\par \par \uc2 One-month NDFs <INR1MNDFOR=> were quoted at 46.52 per dollar, compared to 46.20 on Wednesday. The spot rupee <INR=IN> was also weaker at 46.47.\par \par \uc2 "I think the NDFs are giving up a bit on the Asian appreciation story due to the fears of the likelihood of capital controls plus China also seems to have got through the Obama visit without the exchange rate issue being raised," said a senior trader at a private bank in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 "The onshore market was never a part of this speculation I reckon, so it hasn't reacted much," he said.\par \par \uc2 India has the tools to deal with an influx of foreign capital inflows if they become disruptive, but they are not a concern yet, Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said on Wednesday. [ID:nBOM506984].\par \par \uc2 "After Brazil last night, there are fears that others in Asia might follow suit," another senior trader with a foreign bank said.\par \par \uc2 RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 The rupiah <IDR=> fell just over 1 percent to 9,510 to the dollar, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen selling dollars to support the local unit.\par \par \uc2 In offshore NDFs, the rupiah fell across the curve against the dollar as hedge funds and other investors took advantage of the market jitters to take profits from its recent rally, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Three-month dollar/rupiah NDFs <IDR3MNDFOR=> jumped from Wednesday's 9,485 to 9,625, implying a 1.2 percent rupiah fall from the spot.\par \par \uc2 "With the Brazil news and risk reversion, people got spooked," said a NDF trader in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "BI (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is sporadically capping the dollar/rupiah at so the move in the NDF market has slowed down... 9,510 high was paid but seeing offers slowly coming out," the trader added.\par \par \uc2 Market fears over possible capital curbs by Indonesia have eased after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  played down the threat but investor sentiment was hurt by Brazil's move to curb capital inflows, traders said.\par \par \uc2 PESO\par \par \uc2 The peso <PHP=> fell 0.3 percent to 46.92 per dollar, but the weakness could be temporary as investors awaited fresh capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 "Stocks across Asia right now are mixed, with the major bourses in the red. Commodities are likely to follow suit, and a recovery for the dollar is in play," said a Manila-based dealer.\par \par \uc2 "Dollar/peso could nudge up towards 47.00-10 if the lack of fresh news continues, but afterwards we're still expecting a test towards low 46 levels when the remittance flows peak," the dealer added.\par \par \uc2 Three-month dollar/peso NDFs <PHP3MNDFOR=> edged up to 47.19, implying a 0.6 percent peso fall from the spot.\par \par \uc2 "All tenors are moving to the right. This is normal in squeeze times," said a NDF trader in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=> fell about 0.4 percent against a broadly firmer U.S. dollar to 3.3780.\par \par \uc2 "Early dollar buying below 3.3700 caught the market short, while the euro came under selling pressure, which weighed on the British pound and Australian dollar," a Kuala Lumpur-based dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Traders expect the ringgit to range 3.36 to 3.39 on Thursday and Friday provided euro stays in the 1.4850-1.500 per dollar range.\par \par \uc2 BAHT\par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=> eased to 33.25 per dollar in listless trade, pressured by the firm dollar. The dollar was supported by weaker than expected U.S. housing starts data [ID:nCAT004942], which played into the dollar's hands by reducing risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars in early dealings when the baht was firmer. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out baht fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 "The latest weaker-than-expected U.S. housing starts data hit risk asset appetite in favour of dollar. Baht should move in the 33.16-33.22 range today," said a Bangkok-based trader.\par \par \uc2 The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen 0.4 percent in the past week and 4.91 percent so far this year. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0547 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        89.12          89.37       +0.28\par  Sing dlr        1.3875         1.3838       -0.27\par  Taiwan dlr      32.188         32.128       -0.19\par  Korean won     1156.50        1153.00       -0.30\par  Baht             33.18          33.17       -0.03\par  Peso             46.90          46.78       -0.25\par  Rupiah         9510.00        9380.00       -1.37\par  Rupee            46.45          46.20       -0.53\par  Ringgit         3.3820         3.3640       -0.53\par  Yuan            6.8277         6.8270       -0.01\par  Change so far in 2009\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        89.12          90.60       +1.66\par  Sing dlr        1.3875         1.4340       +3.35\par  Taiwan dlr      32.188         32.860       +2.09\par  Korean won     1156.50        1259.50       +8.91\par  Baht             33.18          34.80       +4.88\par  Peso             46.90          47.52       +1.33\par  Rupiah         9510.00       11000.00      +15.67\par  Rupee            46.45          48.71       +4.88\par  Ringgit         3.3820         3.4500       +2.01\par  Yuan            6.8277         6.8230       -0.07\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Vithoon Amorn in BANGKOK, Swati Bhat in MUMBAI)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091122e5bj000