\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Thai PM: No Need For Rate Hike; Can Only Slow Baht's Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 609 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2009\par 02:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds prime minister's comments on baht, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Prime Minister Abhisit Vejjajiva said in an interview broadcast Monday that there is no need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates, while acknowledging that the authorities can only temper, but not stop, the baht's rise.\par \par \uc2 Thai prices have just turned positive from year-earlier levels after falling "throughout the year, so there's no immediate threat" of inflation, Abhisit told CNBC, according to a transcript of the interview. "Obviously we are not complacent, we are careful, but I don't think it's cause for a tighter monetary policy just yet."\par \par \uc2 On the issue of Asia's rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which Abhisit and other Pacific Rim leaders had discussed at a summit in Singapore over the weekend, the premier said this was "more of a dollar problem than the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this region. There's a lot of pressure on exporters obviously, but we are after all still running quite a substantial current-account surplus, so it's not surprising that the movement is still going the way it is."\par \par \uc2 Heads of some governments in the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation group complained at the summit that the falling dollar and the Chinese yuan--which Beijing informally links to the dollar--were hurting their the export-reliant economies, but Sunday's APEC communique dropped any reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . One participant said this was because China balked at any mention.\par \par \uc2 Abhisit told CNBC that yuan policy is "up to the Chinese to decide," but suggested he'd like China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise. "I am sure that if there's a fundamental imbalance, it would have to be corrected one way or another."\par \par \uc2 As for the baht, Abhisit said the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying "\b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are aimed at reducing volatility. They are not aimed at specific targets and they are certainly not aimed, or able to actually go, against the underlying fundamentals."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought some $15 billion this year to curb the baht's rise, Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij told Dow Jones last week. The dollar slipped to THB33.245 Monday, and there was suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Abhisit told CNBC he expects the Thai economy to grow from year-earlier levels this quarter and said "most estimates" now call for growth of 3.5% next year. In an interview Saturday with Dow Jones Newswires, Abhisit said that growth rate was "achievable."\par \par \uc2 Asked about potential risks to the Thai economy, Abhisit cited the fragility of the global recovery and "any potential problems in terms of politics that can affect the confidence of people, investors," according to CNBC.\par \par \uc2 Abhisit said it's "Up to Cambodia to correct" a bilateral row, in which Phnom Penh has recently appointed Thailand's ex-premier Thaksin Shinawatra as an economic adviser. Thaksin is on self-imposed exile to avoid arrest in Thailand, where he is wanted on corruption charges.\par \par \uc2 Thaksin's appointment has soured recent bilateral cooperation as well as cooperation within the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, Abhisit said. "Why not go back to where we were before?"\par \par \uc2 "We don't intend to let this escalate into any kind of violence, and certainly we have refrained from taking any measures that would have affect on the people--both sides of the border," Abhisit told CNBC. He said he wants to solve the problem bilaterally and not involve Asean.\par \par \uc2 -By Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; djnews.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com [ 16-11-09 0141GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091116e5bg0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
