\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Thai c.bank signals more  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2 , holds rates\b0\par\par\uc2 830 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2009\par 08:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  signals more \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Policy rate unchanged at 1.25 pct, as expected\par \par \uc2 * BOT says rate appropriate, supports economy\par \par \uc2 * Economy is expected to recover gradually - BOT\par \par \uc2 * Most economists see rate rising from Q2 2010 (Adds analyst comments)\par \par \uc2 By Kitiphong Thaichareon and Orathai Sriring\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Oct 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signalled on Wednesday it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb excessive strength in the baht, but that it would not stand in the way of a rising trend so long as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains competitive with its peers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  said the baht's strength was not impeding exports or an economic recovery, although it decided to leave its policy interest rate unchanged at a record low of 1.25 percent for the fourth straight meeting to support growth.\par \par \uc2 It maintained that its policy settings were appropriate and supportive of a recovery from the global downturn, underlining expectations rates would be on hold well into 2010.\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> is up about 4 percent against the dollar this year, making it the fourth-strongest in Asia among the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  monitored daily by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "The current baht is not an obstacle to exports and the economic recovery," \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  (BOT) Assistant Governor Paiboon Kittisrikangwan told a news conference.\par \par \uc2 "We will ensure its smooth moves, but we do not aim to make it stand still all the time."\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are climbing fast off the back of a flood of portfolio investment into emerging markets, which are expected to recover from the global financial crisis before developed economies.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in Thailand, South Korea, Indonesia, Taiwan are among those which have been spotted by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers buying dollars in recent weeks to control their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to help keep exports competitive.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments signalled further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb volatility. But equally, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would allow the baht to rise if it remained in line with economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping up rhetoric of its policy stance regarding the baht. However, it does mean the baht has scope to climb further from here in the event regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  climb as well," said economist Carl Rajoo at Forecast in Singapore\par \par \uc2 The baht, which was at 33.42 baht per dollar on Wednesday against 33.38 on Tuesday. It fell last week to a two-week low on investor concerns about the health of Thailand's 81-year-old king.\par \par \uc2 The baht trails only behind Indonesia's rupiah <IDR=>, South Korea's won <KRW=> and India's rupee <INR=> in levels of appreciation this year. [ID:nSP505836]\par \par \uc2 "For competitiveness, we have to look at competitors' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the baht is still 1 percent weaker against them," Paiboon said, noting that Thai exports had been strengthening despite gains in the baht.\par \par \uc2 As if to underscore his point, data on Wednesday showed exports in September fell a smaller-than-expected 8.5 percent from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 It was the smallest percentage drop since November. At $14.9 billion, the value of exports was also the highest for a single month in a year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said domestic conditions had improved for manufacturing, exports and tourism.\par \par \uc2 All three sectors got a lift from Thailand's loose monetary policy and aggressive fiscal stimulus. However, it said there remained some uncertainties about the domestic and global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 CAUTIOUS\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cautious outlook meant that it would probably keep rates on hold until well into 2010, economists said, similar to their view before Wednesday's policy review.\par \par \uc2 "The BOT remains cautious on growth prospects," said economist Usara Wilaipich, economist at Standard Chartered Bank. "We continue to believe that the BOT is likely to stay put with its monetary policy for much of 2010."\par \par \uc2 Rates are on hold across Asia as central banks wait for clear signs economic recovery has taken hold. One fear policymakers have is that once they start to withdraw the extraordinary stimulus put in place during the downturn, economies will slide back into a downturn.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  cut its rate four times and by a total of 2.50 percentage points between December and April.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it was assessing the impact of earlier cuts and government spending that helped the economy emerge from recession in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 Still, the downturn has done damage. The BOT expects the economy to shrink 3.0-4.5 percent this year, the weakest since 1998, before growing 3-5 percent in 2010.\par \par \uc2 It predicts consumer prices this year would be unchanged to down 1.5 percent before rising 3.5-5.5 percent next year. It reviews its quarterly forecasts on Oct. 29. ($1=33.42 Baht) (Editing by Jason Szep and Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ECONOMY/RATES (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|ABX|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091021e5al000zy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
