\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Peso, baht ease; Indonesia says no target on rupiah\b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2009\par 07:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Peso, baht retreat as Bernanke's comment boosts dollar\par \par \uc2 * Indonesia says it has no target on high-flying rupiah\par \par \uc2 * Singapore dollar pulls back ahead of policy review\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 9 (Reuters) - The Philippine peso and Thai baht drifted lower on Friday as investors bought U.S. dollars after Federal Reserve chief Ben Bernanke indicated monetary policy might have to be tightened as a recovery takes hold.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, several Asian central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit multi-month highs, fearing their exports would become less competitive, but Indonesia's Finance Minister said on Friday that the country is not targeting a specific level for the rupiah. [ID:nJAK191846]\par \par \uc2 PESO\par \par \uc2 The peso <PHP=> eased to 46.61 per dollar, down a third of a percent from its year-high on Thursday,\par \par \uc2 "We are seeing some short covering," said a Manila-based dealer, adding: "Most players sold (dollars) at 46.45 and were expecting the peso to open much lower given the developments overnight."\par \par \uc2 Still, the peso has gained 4.6 percent since the start of September, outperforming most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  except the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> and South Korean <KRW=>.\par \par \uc2 "The peso is in the course of playing catch-up after a period of underperformance. Its valuation, macro-dynamics and balance of payments justify ongoing support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but not outperformance," Stewart Newnham and Yee Wai Chong, analysts at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The analysts expected the peso to rise to 46.0 per dollar by the end of 2009 and 44.0 by end-2010.\par \par \uc2 BAHT\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> eased as part of an Asia-wide retreat.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, several Asian central banks, including those in \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and South Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying dollars to temper the rapid rise in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The baht eased just below its late Thursday level this morning after almost all Asian central banks, including Thailand's, came out yesterday trying to restrain their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But I think the bearish dollar tone is still intact," a dealer at a Thai bank said.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said Thai exporters might pause in selling dollars, saying it has probably been oversold in the last four days.\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR, RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 Both Singapore dollar <SGD=> and Malaysian ringgit retreated after recent gains, tracking euro weakness against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <EUR=>.\par \par \uc2 "Dollar/ringgit is slightly higher but moves in a small range while U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar is also up," said a trader.\par \par \uc2 "The market will watch the euro and Aussie for clues to see if people take profit ahead of long weekend in the United States and Japan," the trader added.\par \par \uc2 Singapore dollar lost a quarter of a percent to 1.3927 per U.S. dollar while ringgit <MYR=> eased almost 0.3 percent to 3.398 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to maintain its zero appreciation policy next Monday. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0547 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        89.23          88.41       -0.92\par  Sing dlr        1.3921         1.3890       -0.22\par  Taiwan dlr      32.221         32.143       -0.24\par  Korean won     1164.75        1167.00       +0.19\par  Baht             33.30          33.31       +0.03\par  Peso             46.48          46.46       -0.04\par  Rupiah         9435.00        9430.00       -0.05\par  Rupee            46.42          46.34       -0.17\par  Ringgit         3.3920         3.3885       -0.10\par  Yuan            6.8256         6.8263       +0.01\par  Change so far in 2009\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        89.23          90.60       +1.54\par  Sing dlr        1.3921         1.4340       +3.01\par  Taiwan dlr      32.221         32.860       +1.98\par  Korean won     1164.75        1259.50       +8.13\par  Baht             33.30          34.80       +4.50\par  Peso             46.48          47.52       +2.24\par  Rupiah         9435.00       11000.00      +16.59\par  Rupee            46.42          48.71       +4.93\par  Ringgit         3.3920         3.4500       +1.71\par  Yuan            6.8256         6.8230       -0.04\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Vithoon Amorn in Bangkok) (Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|ABX|RNP|DNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091012e5a90009z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
